-- One transaction: if any essay was edited since the snapshot, nothing in this part is written.
BEGIN;

-- accrued-expenses-received-not-invoiced
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>An accrued expense is a liability for goods or services already received that the entity has not yet paid, been invoiced for, or formally agreed with the supplier. It sits between a trade payable, where the invoice fixes the amount and date, and a provision, where timing or amount is genuinely uncertain.</p></li><li><p>Accruals exist because of the accrual basis. An expense belongs to the period in which the entity consumed the goods or services, so at every cut-off date the entity records what it received but has not yet been billed for, and next period the invoice replaces the estimate.</p></li><li><p>Most accruals are mechanical: interest by the day, wages by the day worked, rig and vessel hire by the day rate, utilities by the meter. Some need estimates (bonuses, unbilled contractor work), but the uncertainty is small. When it stops being small, the item belongs to PSAK 237 as a provision.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report accrued expenses of 1,897,743 at 31 December 2024 against 2,060,218 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; "Daftar" is the printed page). The line is 6.2 percent of total assets and 30.8 percent of current liabilities (6.7 and 34.2 percent a year earlier). It is PHE's largest current liability, larger than trade payables (1,091,822) and the current portion of long-term bank loans (1,551,034).</p><p>After reading it a student should be able to say when an accrual is needed and how much to record, write the adjusting and reversing entries, tell an accrual from a payable and a provision, and read an accruals note.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.</p><h2>1. What an accrued expense is</h2><p>An accrued expense is a liability, so it must meet the Conceptual Framework's definition: a present obligation of the entity to transfer an economic resource as a result of past events. For an accrual the past event is receiving goods or services. Once a contractor's crew has worked a day on a platform, the entity owes for that day whether or not anyone has sent a bill.</p><p>Current liabilities have three traits: they arise from past events, they fall due within a year, and settling them takes an outflow of cash or other resources. Accrued expenses are the clearest case of all three.</p><p>PSAK 237 <em>Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets</em> (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37) gives the definition that separates accruals from their two neighbours (para. 11). Trade payables are liabilities for goods or services received that have been invoiced or formally agreed with the supplier. Accruals are liabilities for goods or services received that have not yet been paid, invoiced or formally agreed, including amounts due to employees such as accrued holiday pay. Estimating an accrual is sometimes necessary, "but the uncertainty is generally much less than for provisions".</p><p>Take PHE's 2024 balance sheet as the starting point for the three-way split.</p><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Trade payable</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Accrued expense</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Provision</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Goods or services received?</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>yes</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>yes</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>not required (the obligating event can be a law or a past action)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Invoice or formal agreement?</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>yes</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Amount</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>fixed by the invoice</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>contract rate × quantity received, sometimes estimated</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>best estimate of an uncertain outflow</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Timing</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>due date on the invoice</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>when the invoice arrives and is paid, usually weeks</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>uncertain, sometimes decades</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Standard</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 109</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 109, or PSAK 219 for employees</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 237</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PHE line, 31 Dec 2024</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Trade payables, 1,091,822 (Note 19)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Accrued expenses, 1,897,743 (Note 23)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Decommissioning and site restoration, 3,826,222 (Note 29)</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The Trade payables and Decommissioning provision essays cover the outer columns.</p><p>Accrued expenses are current. PSAK 201 (formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1) treats trade payables and accruals for employee and other operating costs as part of the working capital used in the normal operating cycle, so they are current even if some are settled after twelve months (para. 70).</p><h2>2. Why accruals exist: the accrual basis and cut-off</h2><p>PSAK 201 requires financial statements, except cash flow information, to be prepared on the accrual basis (paras. 27–28). Under it an expense is recognised when the entity consumes the resource, not when cash leaves. A <em>cash basis</em> records the expense when paid. The two give different profits whenever service and payment fall in different periods.</p><p>The point at which one period ends and the next begins is the <em>cut-off</em>. Every receipt of goods or services must land on the right side of it. The accrual is the entry that pulls a December service into December when its invoice will only arrive in January.</p><p>For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary hires a well-intervention vessel for a routine workover, a repair that keeps an existing well producing, at a day rate of 150. The vessel works from 20 to 31 December 2024, twelve days. The owner invoices on 15 January 2025 and is paid on 14 February 2025.</p><blockquote><p>Service received in 2024 = 12 days × 150 = 1,800</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash basis</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Accrual basis</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Expense in 2024</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,800</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Liability at 31 Dec 2024</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>none</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>accrued expenses 1,800</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Expense in 2025</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,800</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>On the cash basis 2024 profit is overstated by 1,800 and 2025 profit understated by the same amount. The total over two years is the same; the timing is wrong in both years.</p><p>Kieso explains adjusting entries through <em>matching</em>. The December oil is recognised as December revenue, so the cost of keeping the well running belongs there too. Under PSAK an item must still meet the definition of a liability, but for accruals the two tests agree.</p><h2>3. Recognition and measurement</h2><p><strong>When.</strong> An accrual is recognised when the goods or services are received, which means when the entity obtains control of the goods or when the supplier performs the service. Kieso's goods-in-transit rule from the inventory chapter applies to the liability side too. Goods bought FOB shipping point are owed for once they are handed to the carrier, so a purchase at sea on 31 December is both inventory and a liability, and if the invoice has not arrived it is an accrual.</p><p><strong>How much.</strong> Most accruals need no estimate, only arithmetic. A contract fixes a rate, and records show the quantity received.</p><blockquote><p>Accrued expense = contracted rate × quantity received in the period − amounts already invoiced or paid</p></blockquote><p>The quantity comes from the ordinary paperwork of an operation, namely day-rate logs and daily drilling reports for rigs and vessels, timesheets for crews, meter readings for power and fuel, and goods received notes (GRN) for materials. Purchasing systems use a <em>three-way match</em>, in which the purchase order sets the price, the GRN proves receipt and the invoice confirms the amount. When the GRN is posted before the invoice arrives, the system books the difference to a "goods received not invoiced" account, which is an accrual by another name.</p><p><strong>Measurement basis.</strong> Most accruals to suppliers and lenders are financial liabilities under PSAK 109 <em>Financial Instruments</em> (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9). They are measured at fair value on initial recognition and at amortised cost afterwards, but because they are settled within weeks, discounting changes nothing material and the undiscounted contract amount is used. PHE states this directly. Accruals are carried at "notional amounts", which approximate fair value (Note 3g(ii), Daftar 5/46, PDF p. 63). Accruals to employees follow PSAK 219 <em>Employee Benefits</em> (formerly PSAK 24, IAS 19), which measures short-term benefits undiscounted (para. 11). Amounts owed to the government under statute, such as withheld income tax, are not contractual and so are not financial liabilities at all.</p><h2>4. Typical accruals</h2><p><strong>Interest.</strong> Interest accrues by the day, but is paid on fixed dates that rarely match the reporting date. Interest on a 12 percent note payable, for instance, is accrued at each reporting date. For example (illustration), a subsidiary has a term loan of 500,000 at 6 percent a year, with interest paid semi-annually on 30 April and 31 October.</p><blockquote><p>Interest accrued 1 November to 31 December = 500,000 × 6% × 2/12 = 5,000</p><p>Interest paid 30 April 2025 = 500,000 × 6% × 6/12 = 15,000, of which 5,000 belongs to 2024 and 10,000 to 2025</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>31 Dec 2024: Dr Interest expense 5,000 / Cr Interest payable 5,000</p><p>30 Apr 2025: Dr Interest payable 5,000, Dr Interest expense 10,000 / Cr Cash 15,000</p></blockquote><p>Under the effective interest method the expense also includes amortised transaction costs; the Bank loans essay covers that. PHE's policy records accrued interest "separately from the associated borrowings within the current liabilities section" (Note 3g(ii), Daftar 5/46, PDF p. 63), without saying which line holds it.</p><p><strong>Salaries and wages.</strong> Pay periods rarely end on 31 December. For example (illustration), a field crew earns 500 a week for Monday to Friday and is paid each Friday. 31 December 2024 is a Tuesday, so two days of the week have been worked and not paid.</p><blockquote><p>Accrued wages = 500 × 2/5 = 200</p></blockquote><p>On payday the employer withholds amounts the employee owes to others. Typical examples are income tax, union dues and BPJS, Indonesia's social security contributions. Suppose (illustration) income tax article 21 is withheld at, say, 5 percent, and the employee's BPJS Ketenagakerjaan old-age (JHT) contribution at 2 percent of the week's gross pay.</p><blockquote><p>Withholding tax = 500 × 5% = 25; BPJS = 500 × 2% = 10; net pay = 500 − 25 − 10 = 465</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>31 Dec 2024: Dr Salaries and wages expense 200 / Cr Salaries and wages payable 200</p><p>3 Jan 2025: Dr Salaries and wages payable 200, Dr Salaries and wages expense 300 / Cr Withholding tax payable 25, Cr BPJS payable 10, Cr Cash 465</p></blockquote><p>The 25 and 10 are now owed to the tax office and BPJS. The employer's own BPJS contributions, which Kieso treats as payroll taxes, are accrued the same way, as an expense with a liability until paid. The Other taxes payable essay follows the withholding tax from there.</p><p><strong>Paid absences and bonuses.</strong> Amounts owed to employees are accruals in the PSAK 237 sense, and PSAK 219 itself calls the short-term liability an accrued expense (para. 11(a)). Paid absences and bonuses follow their own recognition rules in PSAK 219, which the Employee benefits essay teaches. PHE carries them on a separate line, Short-term employee benefits, 409,225 of employee incentives at the end of 2024 (Note 25a, Daftar 5/119, PDF p. 136).</p><p><strong>Utilities and operating services.</strong> The December power bill arrives in January, so the cost is accrued from the meter reading. The same logic covers most of an upstream operation, where production expenses alone were 4,647,647 in 2024, about 12,700 a day. Rigs, vessels, helicopters, catering, well services and seismic crews work through December and invoice weeks later. Section 8 shows that this kind of accrual makes up almost all of PHE's line.</p><h2>5. Adjusting and reversing entries</h2><p>The accrual is an <em>adjusting entry</em>, made at the reporting date to bring the books onto the accrual basis. When the invoice arrives next period, the liability must move off accrued expenses and the expense must not be counted twice. Kieso gives two ways of doing it.</p><p>Return to the vessel. Suppose the January invoice comes in at 1,850, not 1,800, because the contract adds a fuel surcharge based on the fuel actually burned, which the owner reports only with the invoice; the subsidiary had estimated it at nil.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Without reversing entry</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>With reversing entry</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2024, adjust</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Production expenses 1,800 / Cr Accrued expenses 1,800</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Production expenses 1,800 / Cr Accrued expenses 1,800</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1 Jan 2025, reverse</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no entry</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Accrued expenses 1,800 / Cr Production expenses 1,800</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>15 Jan 2025, invoice</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Accrued expenses 1,800, Dr Production expenses 50 / Cr Trade payables 1,850</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Production expenses 1,850 / Cr Trade payables 1,850</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>14 Feb 2025, pay</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 1,850 / Cr Cash 1,850</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 1,850 / Cr Cash 1,850</p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>2025 expense with reversal = −1,800 + 1,850 = 50, the same as without</p></blockquote><p>The results are identical. The reversing entry lets the payables clerk post every invoice the same way without checking which ones were accrued; without it each invoice must be matched to its accrual.</p><p>An accrual begins as an estimate, becomes a trade payable when the invoice fixes the amount, and leaves when cash is paid. An entity with a long invoice lag carries more on accrued expenses and less on trade payables, with no difference in what it owes.</p><h2>6. Estimates, errors and the provision boundary</h2><p><strong>Invoices after year end.</strong> The invoice arrived on 15 January, before the statements were authorised on 28 March 2025 (Note 1c, Daftar 5/8, PDF p. 25). Under PSAK 210 <em>Events after the Reporting Period</em> (formerly PSAK 8, IAS 10) it is an adjusting event, because it gives evidence about a condition at the reporting date, the service received in December, so a material difference must be booked in 2024. Closing teams do this by truing up accruals for invoices received during the closing period; an immaterial difference such as this 50 is commonly left to fall into 2025, as in the table. Information that arrives after authorisation is a change in estimate under PSAK 208 (formerly PSAK 25, IAS 8), recognised in the period of the revision without restatement. A surcharge that was in the contract all along and simply missed would be an error, not an estimate.</p><p><strong>Cut-off errors.</strong> A missed accrual behaves like the inventory error in the Inventories essay. Suppose (illustration) the vessel's 1,800 had not been accrued at all and the invoice was expensed in 2025.</p><blockquote><p>2024: expense understated 1,800, profit overstated 1,800, liabilities understated 1,800</p><p>2025: expense overstated 1,800, profit understated 1,800, balance sheet correct</p></blockquote><p>The error reverses over two years, but both years are misstated. If material and found after the 2024 statements were issued, it is corrected retrospectively under PSAK 208 by restating the comparatives. Auditors therefore search for unrecorded liabilities by tracing payments and invoices received after year end back to the period of service.</p><p><strong>When an accrual becomes a provision.</strong> The definition draws the line at the degree of uncertainty, not at the existence of an estimate. For example (illustration), the vessel owner claims an extra 600 of standby charges for weather days in December, which the subsidiary disputes under the contract.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,850 day-rate invoice</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>600 disputed standby claim</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Service received?</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>yes</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>yes, the vessel stood by</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Amount</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>contract rate × logged days</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>depends on the contract reading, perhaps on arbitration</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Timing</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>normal payment terms</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>unknown</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Classification</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>accrual, then trade payable</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>provision if an outflow is probable and can be estimated; otherwise a contingent liability disclosed in the notes</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The Decommissioning provision essay teaches the PSAK 237 rules for the second column.</p><h2>7. Accrued expenses across industries</h2><p><strong>Banks.</strong> For a bank the main accrual is interest owed to depositors. A time deposit's interest builds up daily and is paid at maturity or monthly. For example (illustration), a customer places 10,000 for three months at 5 percent a year on 1 December, with interest at maturity, using an actual/365 day count.</p><blockquote><p>Interest accrued to 31 December = 10,000 × 5% × 31/365 = 42.47</p><p>Dr Interest expense 42.47 / Cr Interest payable 42.47</p></blockquote><p>Indonesian banks commonly show it as "bunga yang masih harus dibayar", next to the deposits on which it accrues.</p><p><strong>Islamic banks.</strong> A mudharabah depositor is entitled to a share of profit, not to interest, and the deposits themselves are temporary syirkah funds (dana syirkah temporer), presented outside liabilities. The liability is the share of profit allocated to depositors and not yet paid, shown as profit sharing not yet distributed, under PSAK 405 <em>Mudharabah</em> (formerly PSAK 105).</p><p><strong>Construction and service contracts.</strong> When revenue is recognised over time using costs incurred as the measure of progress (an input method under PSAK 115, formerly PSAK 72, IFRS 15), a missed cost accrual understates revenue as well as cost. For example (illustration), a contract priced at 10,000 has total estimated cost of 8,000. By year end the subcontractor has been invoiced for 3,000, and has done a further 1,000 of work not yet invoiced.</p><blockquote><p>Without the accrual: progress = 3,000 / 8,000 = 37.5%; revenue = 10,000 × 37.5% = 3,750</p><p>With the accrual: progress = 4,000 / 8,000 = 50.0%; revenue = 10,000 × 50.0% = 5,000</p></blockquote><p>Margin is 750 in the first case (3,750 − 3,000) and 1,000 in the second (5,000 − 4,000). The cut-off failure moves revenue as well as cost, so in contract businesses accruals carry far more weight than their balance suggests.</p><p><strong>Utilities and telecom.</strong> Power distributors and telecom operators accrue purchased power or interconnection charges from meter and traffic data before the counterparty's monthly statement arrives.</p><p><strong>Joint operations.</strong> In oil and gas, mining and infrastructure consortia, the operator incurs costs and bills the partners through a joint interest billing (JIB) statement, usually in the following month. Under PSAK 111 <em>Joint Arrangements</em> (formerly PSAK 66, IFRS 11) a joint operator recognises its share of the liabilities incurred jointly (para. 20), so a non-operator must accrue its share of costs incurred by the operator in December even before the JIB arrives. Section 9 shows how PHE divides these between lines.</p><h2>8. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 23</h2><p>Operating accruals make up 97.7 percent of the line at the end of 2024 (Note 23, Daftar 5/117, PDF p. 134).</p><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2023</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Change</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Accrued expenses involving the subsidiaries' oil and gas operations</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,854,008</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,759,435</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>+94,573</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Overlifting gas</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>11,322</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>237,150</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>−225,828</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Others</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>32,413</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>63,633</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>−31,220</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,897,743</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>2,060,218</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>−162,475</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Operations share = 1,854,008 / 1,897,743 = 97.7% (2024); 1,759,435 / 2,060,218 = 85.4% (2023)</p><p>Change in total = −162,475 / 2,060,218 = −7.9%; operations +5.4%; overlifting gas −95.2%</p></blockquote><p>The line is almost entirely the operating accruals of section 4. The fall in the total comes from overlifting gas (section 9); underneath it the operating accruals rose 5.4 percent.</p><p>PHE's policy treats accrued expenses as financial liabilities at amortised cost, carried at notional amounts (Note 3g(ii), Daftar 5/44 and 5/46, PDF pp. 61, 63), and states that their carrying amount approximates fair value because of their short-term nature (Note 23). The full 1,897,743 appears among the financial liabilities in Note 47 (Daftar 5/222, PDF p. 239).</p><p>Overlifting is not a cost received from a supplier, so the ratios below use the operating accruals alone. Billed amounts owed to operators sit in other payables (429,695 and 300,799, Note 21), so they join trade payables on the billed side.</p><blockquote><p>Operating accruals / (trade payables + operations payables) = 1,854,008 / (1,091,822 + 429,695) = 1.22 (2024); 1,759,435 / (883,688 + 300,799) = 1.49 (2023)</p></blockquote><p>A ratio above 1 means more of what PHE owes for goods and services sits unbilled than billed, which fits services performed daily in the field and billed through monthly cycles.</p><blockquote><p>Cash operating costs = production 4,647,647 + exploration 465,422 + general and administrative 906,779 + other operations 867,866 − dry holes 156,746 = 6,730,968 (2024); 6,061,012 − 100,703 = 5,960,309 (2023)</p><p>Days of accrued costs = 1,854,008 / 6,730,968 × 365 = 100.5 days (2024); 1,759,435 / 5,960,309 × 365 = 107.7 days (2023)</p></blockquote><p>Dry holes (Note 36, Daftar 5/149, PDF p. 166) are removed because they are write-offs, not cash costs. The days figure still overstates the true lag, because part of the operating accruals is likely capital spending on wells and facilities, which goes to oil and gas properties and never passes through these expense lines. PHE does not split the accruals between operating and capital spending, so the ratio shows direction only. The unbilled lag shortened by about seven days in 2024. Most of the 162,475 fall in the whole line came from overlifting, which fell by 225,828.</p><p>Note 48 on foreign-currency monetary items lists rupiah "accrued expenses and other provision" of Rp6,611,848 million, equal to 409,225 (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242), and 290,571 for 2023. Both figures equal the employee incentives in Note 25a, so the row appears to be that line under a loose label. Note 48 does not list Note 23, and the FS do not show how much of the 1,897,743 is in rupiah.</p><p>PHE's deferred tax note labels bonus balances both ways, "accrued expense for bonus" (deferred tax asset of 4,479) and "provision for bonus and incentives" (2,738 among the assets and 14,897 among the liabilities), so labels in the notes do not settle the PSAK 237 classification (Note 43e, Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172).</p><h2>9. Upstream note: overlifting and joint-operation billings</h2><p><strong>Overlifting.</strong> In a production sharing contract (PSC) each party is entitled to a share of production, but physical liftings come in cargoes and pipeline nominations that rarely match each party's entitlement at year end. PHE's policy says that lifting above the final entitlement creates a liability (overlifting) and lifting below it a receivable (underlifting). Gas volumes are valued at the price in the gas sales agreement and crude at the annual weighted average price. Because these adjustments "are not transactions with customers", they are recorded in direct cost of sales, not in revenue (Note 3p, Daftar 5/63, PDF p. 80). For example (illustration), a subsidiary's final 2024 gas entitlement is 1,000 thousand MMBTU, it lifted and sold 1,030 thousand MMBTU, and the gas sales agreement price is USD 6 per MMBTU.</p><blockquote><p>Overlifting = (1,030 − 1,000) thousand MMBTU × USD 6 = 180</p><p>Dr Cost of revenue, overlifting adjustment 180 / Cr Accrued expenses, overlifting gas 180</p></blockquote><p>Revenue stays at the 1,030 sold, and the cost line carries the obligation to give back the excess. It is an accrual because the volume and price are fixed by records and contract, and only the timing of settlement is left open. PHE shows overlifting due to the Government in another line, Due to the Government, within a combined "Overlifting &amp; DMO" item of 51,427 (Note 20, Daftar 5/114, PDF p. 131), and the matching underlifting receivable from SKK Migas in Due from the Government (Note 9, Daftar 5/97, PDF p. 114). Note 23 does not name the counterparty of its 11,322. Reading it as amounts owed to PSC partners or buyers is an inference. The Due from the Government essay covers the government side.</p><p><strong>Billed and unbilled.</strong> Note 21 explains that payables from oil and gas operations are classified as other payables because they come from the proportional share of the JIB statements that operators send to PHE's non-operating subsidiaries (Daftar 5/115, PDF p. 132). If PHE's non-operated interests follow the usual sequence, a cost the operator incurs in December would sit in accrued expenses until the JIB arrives, then in other payables (429,695 at the end of 2024), and be settled against cash calls already advanced (cash call advances of 76,196, an asset; Note 15, Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123) or by a top-up payment (amounts under cash calls of 28,933, Note 21). Note 23 does not confirm the first step.</p><h2>10. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/trade-payables">trade-payables</a>: where an accrual goes once the invoice arrives.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/trade-payables">other-payables</a>: holds the JIB-based payables to operators.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/employee-benefits-liabilities-non-current">short-term-employee-benefits</a>: PHE's separate line for amounts owed to employees.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/due-from-government">due-to-government-current</a>: overlifting and DMO owed to the Government.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/due-from-government">due-from-government</a>: the underlifting receivable on the other side.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/decommissioning-site-restoration-provision">decommissioning-site-restoration-provision</a>: the provision side of the boundary.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/production-expenses">production-expenses</a>: where most operating accruals are charged.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets">deferred-tax-assets</a>: Note 43e's bonus balances.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>That the operating accruals of 1,854,008 are mainly costs incurred and not yet invoiced, including the non-operators' share before the JIB arrives, is read from the PSAK 237.11 definition and Note 21. Note 23 gives no further breakdown, and it may also contain capital spending and amounts that are partly estimated.</p></li><li><p>The counterparty of the 11,322 overlifting gas accrual is not stated. PHE also lists all of Note 23 as financial liabilities in Note 47; any overlifting settled by delivering gas rather than cash would not meet the definition of a financial liability, so that classification may be loose. Note 47 also places accrued expenses in an "other financial liabilities" column, while Note 3g(ii) describes them as measured at amortised cost.</p></li><li><p>Note 48's rupiah "accrued expenses and other provision" equals the employee incentives of Note 25a in both years. This page reads it as that line under a loose label, not as information about Note 23.</p></li><li><p>PHE labels bonus balances in Note 43e both as accruals and as provisions. The page treats this as labelling, not as evidence of how PHE classifies them on the balance sheet.</p></li><li><p>The JIB-to-cash-call sequence in section 9 is the usual industry practice, applied to PHE by inference from Notes 15 and 21.</p></li><li><p>The days-of-accrued-costs ratio uses a balance that probably includes capital accruals over an expense base without them; only the direction of change is meaningful.</p></li><li><p>The Islamic banking paragraph (PSAK 405 profit sharing, temporary syirkah funds) is from knowledge and was not checked against the text.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12); Note 1c (Daftar 5/8, PDF p. 25); Note 3g(ii) (Daftar 5/44 and 5/46, PDF pp. 61, 63); Note 3p (Daftar 5/63, PDF p. 80); Note 9 (Daftar 5/97, PDF p. 114); Note 15 (Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123); Note 19 (Daftar 5/113, PDF p. 130); Note 20 (Daftar 5/114, PDF p. 131); Note 21 (Daftar 5/115, PDF p. 132); Note 23 (Daftar 5/117, PDF p. 134); Note 25a (Daftar 5/119, PDF p. 136); Note 29 (Daftar 5/145, PDF p. 162); Note 36 (Daftar 5/149, PDF p. 166); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172); Note 47 (Daftar 5/222, PDF p. 239); Note 48 (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242). Profit or loss lines from the statement of profit or loss. VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk; Indonesian and English columns read for Notes 3g, 3p, 21 and 23).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 237 <em>Provisi, Liabilitas Kontinjensi, dan Aset Kontinjensi</em> (IAS 37), para. 11. VERIFIED (web, AASB 137.11, which reproduces IAS 37.11; ICAI Ind AS 37).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 219 <em>Imbalan Kerja</em> (IAS 19), para. 11. VERIFIED (web, ICAB copy of IAS 19 (2017), as confirmed by the independent recheck).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 201 <em>Penyajian Laporan Keuangan</em> (IAS 1), paras. 27–28, 70; PSAK 208 (IAS 8), para. 5 (errors vs changes in estimate); PSAK 210 (IAS 10), para. 8; PSAK 109 (IFRS 9); PSAK 115 (IFRS 15); PSAK 111 (IFRS 11), para. 20. Numbers VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list, as used across this series); paragraph content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 405 <em>Akuntansi Mudharabah</em> (formerly PSAK 105). Number VERIFIED (web, secondary academic sources citing PSAK 405 from 1 January 2024); content NOT VERIFIED. Monthly PPh 21 withholding under PP 58/2023 (average effective rates) is mentioned only as "say, 5 percent"; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting</em>, IFRS edition, chs. 3 and 13. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An accrued expense is a liability for goods or services already received that the entity has not yet paid, been invoiced for, or formally agreed with the supplier. It sits between a trade payable, where the invoice fixes the amount and date, and a provision, where timing or amount is genuinely uncertain.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accruals exist because of the accrual basis. An expense belongs to the period in which the entity consumed the goods or services, so at every cut-off date the entity records what it received but has not yet been billed for, and next period the invoice replaces the estimate.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Most accruals are mechanical: interest by the day, wages by the day worked, rig and vessel hire by the day rate, utilities by the meter. Some need estimates (bonuses, unbilled contractor work), but the uncertainty is small. When it stops being small, the item belongs to PSAK 237 as a provision.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report accrued expenses of 1,897,743 at 31 December 2024 against 2,060,218 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The line is 6.2 percent of total assets and 30.8 percent of current liabilities (6.7 and 34.2 percent a year earlier). It is PHE's largest current liability, larger than trade payables (1,091,822) and the current portion of long-term bank loans (1,551,034).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading it a student should be able to say when an accrual is needed and how much to record, write the adjusting and reversing entries, tell an accrual from a payable and a provision, and read an accruals note.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. What an accrued expense is","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An accrued expense is a liability, so it must meet the Conceptual Framework's definition: a present obligation of the entity to transfer an economic resource as a result of past events. For an accrual the past event is receiving goods or services. Once a contractor's crew has worked a day on a platform, the entity owes for that day whether or not anyone has sent a bill.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current liabilities have three traits: they arise from past events, they fall due within a year, and settling them takes an outflow of cash or other resources. Accrued expenses are the clearest case of all three.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 237 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37) gives the definition that separates accruals from their two neighbours (para. 11). Trade payables are liabilities for goods or services received that have been invoiced or formally agreed with the supplier. Accruals are liabilities for goods or services received that have not yet been paid, invoiced or formally agreed, including amounts due to employees such as accrued holiday pay. Estimating an accrual is sometimes necessary, \"but the uncertainty is generally much less than for provisions\".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Take PHE's 2024 balance sheet as the starting point for the three-way split.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade payable","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accrued expense","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Provision","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goods or services received?","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"yes","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"yes","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"not required (the obligating event can be a law or a past action)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Invoice or formal agreement?","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"yes","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Amount","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"fixed by the invoice","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"contract rate × quantity received, sometimes estimated","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"best estimate of an uncertain outflow","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Timing","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"due date on the invoice","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"when the invoice arrives and is paid, usually weeks","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"uncertain, sometimes decades","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Standard","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 109","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 109, or PSAK 219 for employees","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 237","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE line, 31 Dec 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade payables, 1,091,822 (Note 19)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accrued expenses, 1,897,743 (Note 23)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Decommissioning and site restoration, 3,826,222 (Note 29)","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The Trade payables and Decommissioning provision essays cover the outer columns.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accrued expenses are current. PSAK 201 (formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1) treats trade payables and accruals for employee and other operating costs as part of the working capital used in the normal operating cycle, so they are current even if some are settled after twelve months (para. 70).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Why accruals exist: the accrual basis and cut-off","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 201 requires financial statements, except cash flow information, to be prepared on the accrual basis (paras. 27–28). Under it an expense is recognised when the entity consumes the resource, not when cash leaves. A ","type":"text"},{"text":"cash basis","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" records the expense when paid. The two give different profits whenever service and payment fall in different periods.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The point at which one period ends and the next begins is the ","type":"text"},{"text":"cut-off","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". Every receipt of goods or services must land on the right side of it. The accrual is the entry that pulls a December service into December when its invoice will only arrive in January.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary hires a well-intervention vessel for a routine workover, a repair that keeps an existing well producing, at a day rate of 150. The vessel works from 20 to 31 December 2024, twelve days. The owner invoices on 15 January 2025 and is paid on 14 February 2025.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Service received in 2024 = 12 days × 150 = 1,800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash basis","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accrual basis","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expense in 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,800","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Liability at 31 Dec 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"none","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"accrued expenses 1,800","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expense in 2025","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"On the cash basis 2024 profit is overstated by 1,800 and 2025 profit understated by the same amount. The total over two years is the same; the timing is wrong in both years.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso explains adjusting entries through ","type":"text"},{"text":"matching","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". The December oil is recognised as December revenue, so the cost of keeping the well running belongs there too. Under PSAK an item must still meet the definition of a liability, but for accruals the two tests agree.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Recognition and measurement","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" An accrual is recognised when the goods or services are received, which means when the entity obtains control of the goods or when the supplier performs the service. Kieso's goods-in-transit rule from the inventory chapter applies to the liability side too. Goods bought FOB shipping point are owed for once they are handed to the carrier, so a purchase at sea on 31 December is both inventory and a liability, and if the invoice has not arrived it is an accrual.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"How much.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Most accruals need no estimate, only arithmetic. A contract fixes a rate, and records show the quantity received.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accrued expense = contracted rate × quantity received in the period − amounts already invoiced or paid","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The quantity comes from the ordinary paperwork of an operation, namely day-rate logs and daily drilling reports for rigs and vessels, timesheets for crews, meter readings for power and fuel, and goods received notes (GRN) for materials. Purchasing systems use a ","type":"text"},{"text":"three-way match","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", in which the purchase order sets the price, the GRN proves receipt and the invoice confirms the amount. When the GRN is posted before the invoice arrives, the system books the difference to a \"goods received not invoiced\" account, which is an accrual by another name.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Measurement basis.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Most accruals to suppliers and lenders are financial liabilities under PSAK 109 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Financial Instruments","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9). They are measured at fair value on initial recognition and at amortised cost afterwards, but because they are settled within weeks, discounting changes nothing material and the undiscounted contract amount is used. PHE states this directly. Accruals are carried at \"notional amounts\", which approximate fair value (Note 3g(ii), Daftar 5/46, PDF p. 63). Accruals to employees follow PSAK 219 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Employee Benefits","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 24, IAS 19), which measures short-term benefits undiscounted (para. 11). Amounts owed to the government under statute, such as withheld income tax, are not contractual and so are not financial liabilities at all.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Typical accruals","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Interest accrues by the day, but is paid on fixed dates that rarely match the reporting date. Interest on a 12 percent note payable, for instance, is accrued at each reporting date. For example (illustration), a subsidiary has a term loan of 500,000 at 6 percent a year, with interest paid semi-annually on 30 April and 31 October.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest accrued 1 November to 31 December = 500,000 × 6% × 2/12 = 5,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest paid 30 April 2025 = 500,000 × 6% × 6/12 = 15,000, of which 5,000 belongs to 2024 and 10,000 to 2025","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024: Dr Interest expense 5,000 / Cr Interest payable 5,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"30 Apr 2025: Dr Interest payable 5,000, Dr Interest expense 10,000 / Cr Cash 15,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Under the effective interest method the expense also includes amortised transaction costs; the Bank loans essay covers that. PHE's policy records accrued interest \"separately from the associated borrowings within the current liabilities section\" (Note 3g(ii), Daftar 5/46, PDF p. 63), without saying which line holds it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Salaries and wages.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Pay periods rarely end on 31 December. For example (illustration), a field crew earns 500 a week for Monday to Friday and is paid each Friday. 31 December 2024 is a Tuesday, so two days of the week have been worked and not paid.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accrued wages = 500 × 2/5 = 200","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"On payday the employer withholds amounts the employee owes to others. Typical examples are income tax, union dues and BPJS, Indonesia's social security contributions. Suppose (illustration) income tax article 21 is withheld at, say, 5 percent, and the employee's BPJS Ketenagakerjaan old-age (JHT) contribution at 2 percent of the week's gross pay.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Withholding tax = 500 × 5% = 25; BPJS = 500 × 2% = 10; net pay = 500 − 25 − 10 = 465","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024: Dr Salaries and wages expense 200 / Cr Salaries and wages payable 200","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3 Jan 2025: Dr Salaries and wages payable 200, Dr Salaries and wages expense 300 / Cr Withholding tax payable 25, Cr BPJS payable 10, Cr Cash 465","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 25 and 10 are now owed to the tax office and BPJS. The employer's own BPJS contributions, which Kieso treats as payroll taxes, are accrued the same way, as an expense with a liability until paid. The Other taxes payable essay follows the withholding tax from there.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Paid absences and bonuses.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Amounts owed to employees are accruals in the PSAK 237 sense, and PSAK 219 itself calls the short-term liability an accrued expense (para. 11(a)). Paid absences and bonuses follow their own recognition rules in PSAK 219, which the Employee benefits essay teaches. PHE carries them on a separate line, Short-term employee benefits, 409,225 of employee incentives at the end of 2024 (Note 25a, Daftar 5/119, PDF p. 136).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Utilities and operating services.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The December power bill arrives in January, so the cost is accrued from the meter reading. The same logic covers most of an upstream operation, where production expenses alone were 4,647,647 in 2024, about 12,700 a day. Rigs, vessels, helicopters, catering, well services and seismic crews work through December and invoice weeks later. Section 8 shows that this kind of accrual makes up almost all of PHE's line.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Adjusting and reversing entries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The accrual is an ","type":"text"},{"text":"adjusting entry","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", made at the reporting date to bring the books onto the accrual basis. When the invoice arrives next period, the liability must move off accrued expenses and the expense must not be counted twice. Kieso gives two ways of doing it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Return to the vessel. Suppose the January invoice comes in at 1,850, not 1,800, because the contract adds a fuel surcharge based on the fuel actually burned, which the owner reports only with the invoice; the subsidiary had estimated it at nil.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Without reversing entry","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"With reversing entry","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024, adjust","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Production expenses 1,800 / Cr Accrued expenses 1,800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Production expenses 1,800 / Cr Accrued expenses 1,800","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1 Jan 2025, reverse","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no entry","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Accrued expenses 1,800 / Cr Production expenses 1,800","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"15 Jan 2025, invoice","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Accrued expenses 1,800, Dr Production expenses 50 / Cr Trade payables 1,850","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Production expenses 1,850 / Cr Trade payables 1,850","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"14 Feb 2025, pay","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 1,850 / Cr Cash 1,850","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 1,850 / Cr Cash 1,850","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025 expense with reversal = −1,800 + 1,850 = 50, the same as without","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The results are identical. The reversing entry lets the payables clerk post every invoice the same way without checking which ones were accrued; without it each invoice must be matched to its accrual.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An accrual begins as an estimate, becomes a trade payable when the invoice fixes the amount, and leaves when cash is paid. An entity with a long invoice lag carries more on accrued expenses and less on trade payables, with no difference in what it owes.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Estimates, errors and the provision boundary","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Invoices after year end.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The invoice arrived on 15 January, before the statements were authorised on 28 March 2025 (Note 1c, Daftar 5/8, PDF p. 25). Under PSAK 210 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Events after the Reporting Period","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 8, IAS 10) it is an adjusting event, because it gives evidence about a condition at the reporting date, the service received in December, so a material difference must be booked in 2024. Closing teams do this by truing up accruals for invoices received during the closing period; an immaterial difference such as this 50 is commonly left to fall into 2025, as in the table. Information that arrives after authorisation is a change in estimate under PSAK 208 (formerly PSAK 25, IAS 8), recognised in the period of the revision without restatement. A surcharge that was in the contract all along and simply missed would be an error, not an estimate.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cut-off errors.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A missed accrual behaves like the inventory error in the Inventories essay. Suppose (illustration) the vessel's 1,800 had not been accrued at all and the invoice was expensed in 2025.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024: expense understated 1,800, profit overstated 1,800, liabilities understated 1,800","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025: expense overstated 1,800, profit understated 1,800, balance sheet correct","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The error reverses over two years, but both years are misstated. If material and found after the 2024 statements were issued, it is corrected retrospectively under PSAK 208 by restating the comparatives. Auditors therefore search for unrecorded liabilities by tracing payments and invoices received after year end back to the period of service.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When an accrual becomes a provision.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The definition draws the line at the degree of uncertainty, not at the existence of an estimate. For example (illustration), the vessel owner claims an extra 600 of standby charges for weather days in December, which the subsidiary disputes under the contract.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,850 day-rate invoice","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"600 disputed standby claim","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Service received?","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"yes","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"yes, the vessel stood by","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Amount","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"contract rate × logged days","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"depends on the contract reading, perhaps on arbitration","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Timing","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"normal payment terms","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"unknown","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Classification","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"accrual, then trade payable","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"provision if an outflow is probable and can be estimated; otherwise a contingent liability disclosed in the notes","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The Decommissioning provision essay teaches the PSAK 237 rules for the second column.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Accrued expenses across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Banks.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" For a bank the main accrual is interest owed to depositors. A time deposit's interest builds up daily and is paid at maturity or monthly. For example (illustration), a customer places 10,000 for three months at 5 percent a year on 1 December, with interest at maturity, using an actual/365 day count.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest accrued to 31 December = 10,000 × 5% × 31/365 = 42.47","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Interest expense 42.47 / Cr Interest payable 42.47","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Indonesian banks commonly show it as \"bunga yang masih harus dibayar\", next to the deposits on which it accrues.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Islamic banks.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A mudharabah depositor is entitled to a share of profit, not to interest, and the deposits themselves are temporary syirkah funds (dana syirkah temporer), presented outside liabilities. The liability is the share of profit allocated to depositors and not yet paid, shown as profit sharing not yet distributed, under PSAK 405 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Mudharabah","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 105).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Construction and service contracts.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" When revenue is recognised over time using costs incurred as the measure of progress (an input method under PSAK 115, formerly PSAK 72, IFRS 15), a missed cost accrual understates revenue as well as cost. For example (illustration), a contract priced at 10,000 has total estimated cost of 8,000. By year end the subcontractor has been invoiced for 3,000, and has done a further 1,000 of work not yet invoiced.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Without the accrual: progress = 3,000 / 8,000 = 37.5%; revenue = 10,000 × 37.5% = 3,750","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"With the accrual: progress = 4,000 / 8,000 = 50.0%; revenue = 10,000 × 50.0% = 5,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Margin is 750 in the first case (3,750 − 3,000) and 1,000 in the second (5,000 − 4,000). The cut-off failure moves revenue as well as cost, so in contract businesses accruals carry far more weight than their balance suggests.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Utilities and telecom.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Power distributors and telecom operators accrue purchased power or interconnection charges from meter and traffic data before the counterparty's monthly statement arrives.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Joint operations.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" In oil and gas, mining and infrastructure consortia, the operator incurs costs and bills the partners through a joint interest billing (JIB) statement, usually in the following month. Under PSAK 111 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Joint Arrangements","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 66, IFRS 11) a joint operator recognises its share of the liabilities incurred jointly (para. 20), so a non-operator must accrue its share of costs incurred by the operator in December even before the JIB arrives. Section 9 shows how PHE divides these between lines.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 23","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Operating accruals make up 97.7 percent of the line at the end of 2024 (Note 23, Daftar 5/117, PDF p. 134).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2023","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Change","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accrued expenses involving the subsidiaries' oil and gas operations","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,854,008","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,759,435","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"+94,573","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Overlifting gas","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11,322","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"237,150","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"−225,828","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Others","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"32,413","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"63,633","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"−31,220","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,897,743","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,060,218","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"−162,475","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Operations share = 1,854,008 / 1,897,743 = 97.7% (2024); 1,759,435 / 2,060,218 = 85.4% (2023)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Change in total = −162,475 / 2,060,218 = −7.9%; operations +5.4%; overlifting gas −95.2%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The line is almost entirely the operating accruals of section 4. The fall in the total comes from overlifting gas (section 9); underneath it the operating accruals rose 5.4 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's policy treats accrued expenses as financial liabilities at amortised cost, carried at notional amounts (Note 3g(ii), Daftar 5/44 and 5/46, PDF pp. 61, 63), and states that their carrying amount approximates fair value because of their short-term nature (Note 23). The full 1,897,743 appears among the financial liabilities in Note 47 (Daftar 5/222, PDF p. 239).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Overlifting is not a cost received from a supplier, so the ratios below use the operating accruals alone. Billed amounts owed to operators sit in other payables (429,695 and 300,799, Note 21), so they join trade payables on the billed side.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Operating accruals / (trade payables + operations payables) = 1,854,008 / (1,091,822 + 429,695) = 1.22 (2024); 1,759,435 / (883,688 + 300,799) = 1.49 (2023)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A ratio above 1 means more of what PHE owes for goods and services sits unbilled than billed, which fits services performed daily in the field and billed through monthly cycles.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash operating costs = production 4,647,647 + exploration 465,422 + general and administrative 906,779 + other operations 867,866 − dry holes 156,746 = 6,730,968 (2024); 6,061,012 − 100,703 = 5,960,309 (2023)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Days of accrued costs = 1,854,008 / 6,730,968 × 365 = 100.5 days (2024); 1,759,435 / 5,960,309 × 365 = 107.7 days (2023)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dry holes (Note 36, Daftar 5/149, PDF p. 166) are removed because they are write-offs, not cash costs. The days figure still overstates the true lag, because part of the operating accruals is likely capital spending on wells and facilities, which goes to oil and gas properties and never passes through these expense lines. PHE does not split the accruals between operating and capital spending, so the ratio shows direction only. The unbilled lag shortened by about seven days in 2024. Most of the 162,475 fall in the whole line came from overlifting, which fell by 225,828.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 48 on foreign-currency monetary items lists rupiah \"accrued expenses and other provision\" of Rp6,611,848 million, equal to 409,225 (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242), and 290,571 for 2023. Both figures equal the employee incentives in Note 25a, so the row appears to be that line under a loose label. Note 48 does not list Note 23, and the FS do not show how much of the 1,897,743 is in rupiah.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's deferred tax note labels bonus balances both ways, \"accrued expense for bonus\" (deferred tax asset of 4,479) and \"provision for bonus and incentives\" (2,738 among the assets and 14,897 among the liabilities), so labels in the notes do not settle the PSAK 237 classification (Note 43e, Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Upstream note: overlifting and joint-operation billings","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Overlifting.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" In a production sharing contract (PSC) each party is entitled to a share of production, but physical liftings come in cargoes and pipeline nominations that rarely match each party's entitlement at year end. PHE's policy says that lifting above the final entitlement creates a liability (overlifting) and lifting below it a receivable (underlifting). Gas volumes are valued at the price in the gas sales agreement and crude at the annual weighted average price. Because these adjustments \"are not transactions with customers\", they are recorded in direct cost of sales, not in revenue (Note 3p, Daftar 5/63, PDF p. 80). For example (illustration), a subsidiary's final 2024 gas entitlement is 1,000 thousand MMBTU, it lifted and sold 1,030 thousand MMBTU, and the gas sales agreement price is USD 6 per MMBTU.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Overlifting = (1,030 − 1,000) thousand MMBTU × USD 6 = 180","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cost of revenue, overlifting adjustment 180 / Cr Accrued expenses, overlifting gas 180","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Revenue stays at the 1,030 sold, and the cost line carries the obligation to give back the excess. It is an accrual because the volume and price are fixed by records and contract, and only the timing of settlement is left open. PHE shows overlifting due to the Government in another line, Due to the Government, within a combined \"Overlifting & DMO\" item of 51,427 (Note 20, Daftar 5/114, PDF p. 131), and the matching underlifting receivable from SKK Migas in Due from the Government (Note 9, Daftar 5/97, PDF p. 114). Note 23 does not name the counterparty of its 11,322. Reading it as amounts owed to PSC partners or buyers is an inference. The Due from the Government essay covers the government side.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Billed and unbilled.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Note 21 explains that payables from oil and gas operations are classified as other payables because they come from the proportional share of the JIB statements that operators send to PHE's non-operating subsidiaries (Daftar 5/115, PDF p. 132). If PHE's non-operated interests follow the usual sequence, a cost the operator incurs in December would sit in accrued expenses until the JIB arrives, then in other payables (429,695 at the end of 2024), and be settled against cash calls already advanced (cash call advances of 76,196, an asset; Note 15, Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123) or by a top-up payment (amounts under cash calls of 28,933, Note 21). Note 23 does not confirm the first step.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"trade-payables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/trade-payables","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where an accrual goes once the invoice arrives.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-payables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/trade-payables","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": holds the JIB-based payables to operators.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"short-term-employee-benefits","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/employee-benefits-liabilities-non-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": PHE's separate line for amounts owed to employees.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"due-to-government-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/due-from-government","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": overlifting and DMO owed to the Government.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"due-from-government","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/due-from-government","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the underlifting receivable on the other side.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"decommissioning-site-restoration-provision","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/decommissioning-site-restoration-provision","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the provision side of the boundary.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"production-expenses","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/production-expenses","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where most operating accruals are charged.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"deferred-tax-assets","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": Note 43e's bonus balances.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That the operating accruals of 1,854,008 are mainly costs incurred and not yet invoiced, including the non-operators' share before the JIB arrives, is read from the PSAK 237.11 definition and Note 21. Note 23 gives no further breakdown, and it may also contain capital spending and amounts that are partly estimated.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The counterparty of the 11,322 overlifting gas accrual is not stated. PHE also lists all of Note 23 as financial liabilities in Note 47; any overlifting settled by delivering gas rather than cash would not meet the definition of a financial liability, so that classification may be loose. Note 47 also places accrued expenses in an \"other financial liabilities\" column, while Note 3g(ii) describes them as measured at amortised cost.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 48's rupiah \"accrued expenses and other provision\" equals the employee incentives of Note 25a in both years. This page reads it as that line under a loose label, not as information about Note 23.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE labels bonus balances in Note 43e both as accruals and as provisions. The page treats this as labelling, not as evidence of how PHE classifies them on the balance sheet.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The JIB-to-cash-call sequence in section 9 is the usual industry practice, applied to PHE by inference from Notes 15 and 21.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The days-of-accrued-costs ratio uses a balance that probably includes capital accruals over an expense base without them; only the direction of change is meaningful.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The Islamic banking paragraph (PSAK 405 profit sharing, temporary syirkah funds) is from knowledge and was not checked against the text.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12); Note 1c (Daftar 5/8, PDF p. 25); Note 3g(ii) (Daftar 5/44 and 5/46, PDF pp. 61, 63); Note 3p (Daftar 5/63, PDF p. 80); Note 9 (Daftar 5/97, PDF p. 114); Note 15 (Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123); Note 19 (Daftar 5/113, PDF p. 130); Note 20 (Daftar 5/114, PDF p. 131); Note 21 (Daftar 5/115, PDF p. 132); Note 23 (Daftar 5/117, PDF p. 134); Note 25a (Daftar 5/119, PDF p. 136); Note 29 (Daftar 5/145, PDF p. 162); Note 36 (Daftar 5/149, PDF p. 166); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172); Note 47 (Daftar 5/222, PDF p. 239); Note 48 (Daftar 5/225, PDF p. 242). Profit or loss lines from the statement of profit or loss. VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk; Indonesian and English columns read for Notes 3g, 3p, 21 and 23).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 237 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Provisi, Liabilitas Kontinjensi, dan Aset Kontinjensi","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 37), para. 11. VERIFIED (web, AASB 137.11, which reproduces IAS 37.11; ICAI Ind AS 37).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 219 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Imbalan Kerja","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 19), para. 11. VERIFIED (web, ICAB copy of IAS 19 (2017), as confirmed by the independent recheck).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 201 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Penyajian Laporan Keuangan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 1), paras. 27–28, 70; PSAK 208 (IAS 8), para. 5 (errors vs changes in estimate); PSAK 210 (IAS 10), para. 8; PSAK 109 (IFRS 9); PSAK 115 (IFRS 15); PSAK 111 (IFRS 11), para. 20. Numbers VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list, as used across this series); paragraph content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 405 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Akuntansi Mudharabah","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 105). Number VERIFIED (web, secondary academic sources citing PSAK 405 from 1 January 2024); content NOT VERIFIED. Monthly PPh 21 withholding under PP 58/2023 (average effective rates) is mentioned only as \"say, 5 percent\"; NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", IFRS edition, chs. 3 and 13. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of accrued expenses from the accrual basis to disclosure, worked through PHE's operating accruals and set beside the industries where accruals drive the numbers","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["An accrued expense is a liability for goods or services already received that the entity has not yet paid, been invoiced for, or formally agreed with the supplier. It sits between a trade payable, where the invoice fixes the amount and date, and a provision, where timing or amount is genuinely uncertain.","Accruals exist because of the accrual basis. An expense belongs to the period in which the entity consumed the goods or services, so at every cut-off date the entity records what it received but has not yet been billed for, and next period the invoice replaces the estimate.","Most accruals are mechanical: interest by the day, wages by the day worked, rig and vessel hire by the day rate, utilities by the meter. Some need estimates (bonuses, unbilled contractor work), but the uncertainty is small. When it stops being small, the item belongs to PSAK 237 as a provision."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = '1dd0a7ad-6098-4615-bf5d-94ca2decb699' AND updated_at = '2026-10-12T09:47:08.318531+00:00'::timestamptz;
