-- business-combinations-acquisition-method-goodwill-common-control
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>A business combination is a transaction in which an acquirer obtains control of a <em>business</em>. Buying a set of assets that is not a business is an asset acquisition: the price is spread over the assets on relative fair values, and no goodwill or bargain purchase gain arises. The optional concentration test settles many cases quickly.</p></li><li><p>Under the acquisition method (PSAK 103) the acquirer measures the consideration and every identifiable asset and liability at acquisition-date fair value, including intangibles the seller never recorded. Goodwill is the residual. A negative residual is a bargain purchase gain in profit or loss, recognised only after the numbers have been rechecked. Provisional amounts may be corrected for up to one year.</p></li><li><p>When the same party controls both sides, nothing is bought from an outsider, so PSAK 338 replaces fair values with carrying amounts (pooling of interests) and puts the difference from the price in additional paid-in capital.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Barito Pacific Tbk and subsidiaries (BRPT) report a gain from bargain purchase of 1,868,039 for 2025, in USD thousand, against profit for the year of 1,615,634 (BRPT FS 2025, statement of profit or loss, PDF p. 17). Two purchases by its petrochemical subsidiary produced it.</p><p>After reading this page a student should be able to classify a purchase, apply the acquisition method, allocate the excess of price over book value, handle the measurement period and record a common-control combination.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from BRPT's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.</p><h2>1. Why companies combine and the legal forms</h2><p>The legal form of a combination decides whose ledger the acquired assets land in, not how they are measured. The motives for combining range from cost savings to tax, and combinations are classified as horizontal (same line of business), vertical and conglomerate (unrelated lines). Vertical integration joins different stages of one supply chain, a refinery buying its crude supplier.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Form</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Formula</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>What the acquirer records in its own books</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Merger</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>A + B = A</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>B's assets and liabilities directly; B is dissolved</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Consolidation</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>A + B = C</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>a new company C takes over both; A and B are dissolved</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Stock acquisition</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>A controls B</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>an investment in B; B keeps its own books and is consolidated</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Measurement is the same for all three. In a merger the values go into A's ledger; in a stock acquisition they appear only on the consolidation worksheet (section 7). BRPT's 2025 purchases were stock acquisitions. CAPGC Pte. Ltd., a subsidiary of PT Chandra Asri Pacific Tbk (CAP), bought 100 percent of the shares of Aster Chemical and Energy Pte. Ltd. (ACE) from Shell Singapore (Note 1d, PDF p. 43).</p><h2>2. What is a business: combination or asset acquisition</h2><p>The acquisition method applies only when what is acquired is a business. PSAK 103 <em>Kombinasi Bisnis</em> (formerly PSAK 22, IFRS 3) scopes out the acquisition of an asset or group of assets that is not a business (para. 2(b)) and combinations of entities under common control (para. 2(c)).</p><p><strong>A business needs an input and a substantive process.</strong> A business is an integrated set of activities and assets capable of being managed to provide goods or services to customers. Outputs are not required, but it needs at least an <em>input</em> and a <em>substantive process</em> that together significantly contribute to the ability to create outputs (para. B8). An operating wind farm with turbines, a power purchase agreement (PPA) and staff is a business; a development licence with no workforce is usually not.</p><p><strong>The concentration test is a shortcut out of the definition.</strong> Since 1 January 2021 an optional test, elected deal by deal (paras. B7A–B7B), applies. If substantially all of the fair value of the gross assets acquired is concentrated in a single identifiable asset or a group of similar assets, the set is not a business and no further analysis is needed. Cash, deferred tax assets and goodwill from deferred tax are left out of gross assets, and a tangible asset and an intangible asset are never "similar".</p><p>For example (illustration), take BRPT's acquisition of 51 percent of PT Lombok Timur Bayu Energi, a wind project company, on 3 January 2024 (Note 43, PDF pp. 231–232).</p><blockquote><p>Gross assets acquired = 1 cash + 44 receivables + 2 other current + 37 fixed assets + 7,700 project development costs = 7,784</p><p>Gross assets excluding cash = 7,783</p><p>Concentration = 7,700 / 7,783 = 98.9%, substantially all in one asset</p></blockquote><p><strong>The boundary changes five things.</strong> Suppose (illustration) the buyer also paid 150 of legal fees and, had the project been a business, the development rights would have been valued independently at 6,078.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Asset acquisition</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Business combination</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Price allocation</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Spread over assets on relative fair values</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Each item at its own fair value</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Goodwill or bargain purchase gain</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Never</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Residual, here 3,122 + 49% × 4,500 − 4,500 = 827 goodwill (before deferred tax)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Transaction costs of 150</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Capitalised into the assets</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Expensed (para. 53)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Deferred tax on book–tax differences</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Not recognised (initial recognition exemption, PSAK 212.15(b))</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Recognised, which changes goodwill</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Contingent payments</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No settled rule; often recognised when the obligation arises</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Fair value at acquisition date (section 4)</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>In the business column, identifiable net assets are 6,122 − 7,700 + 6,078 = 4,500 and the non-controlling interest (NCI) at its proportionate share is 2,205. BRPT's actual entry follows the asset-acquisition column, with NCI of 49% × 6,122 = 3,000 (rounded) and no goodwill.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Project development costs 7,700; Dr Cash 1; Dr Receivables 44; Dr Other current assets 2; Dr Fixed assets 37 / Cr Accrued expenses 962; Cr Taxes payable 52; Cr Trade and other payables 648; Cr NCI 3,000; Cr Cash 3,122</p></blockquote><h2>3. The acquisition method: acquirer and acquisition date</h2><p>Control, not legal form or the signing date, fixes both the acquirer and the acquisition date, the first two of the method's four steps (para. 5).</p><p><strong>The acquirer</strong> is the entity that obtains control under PSAK 110 <em>Laporan Keuangan Konsolidasian</em> (formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10) (para. B13). When that is unclear, the standard looks at who pays, who issues shares, whose owners and managers dominate afterwards, and relative size (paras. B14–B17). In a <em>reverse acquisition</em>, typical of a backdoor listing, the owners of a private company receive enough new shares of a listed shell to control it, so the private company is the accounting acquirer (para. B19).</p><p><strong>The acquisition date</strong> is the date control passes, usually the closing date, which may differ from the signing date (paras. 8–9). It fixes the fair values, the start of consolidation and the measurement period. Shell announced its agreement to sell to CAPGC on 8 May 2024, but control passed at completion on 1 April 2025, the acquisition date in Note 43 (PDF p. 226). Aster Polymer Solutions Pte. Ltd. (APS) follows the same pattern: agreement on 6 May 2025, effective and consolidated from 1 August 2025 (PDF pp. 228–229).</p><h2>4. Consideration transferred and acquisition-related costs</h2><p><strong>Consideration</strong>, the price the acquirer pays, is measured at fair value: cash paid, other assets given up, liabilities incurred to the former owners, and equity issued (para. 37). For example (illustration), Pop Corporation issues 100,000 shares of USD 10 par, market price USD 16, for the net assets of Son.</p><blockquote><p>Consideration = 100,000 × 16 = 1,600,000; common stock = 100,000 × 10 = 1,000,000; paid-in capital = 600,000</p><p>Dr Investment in Son 1,600,000 / Cr Common stock 1,000,000; Cr Paid-in capital 600,000</p></blockquote><p><strong>Acquisition-related costs</strong> are not part of the price. Advisory, legal, accounting, valuation and internal acquisition-department costs are expensed when incurred, and the costs of issuing debt or equity follow PSAK 232 and PSAK 109 (para. 53). In the Pop example the 80,000 consultant fee is expensed, and SEC fees, accountants' fees and share certificates (5,000 + 10,000 + 25,000 = 40,000) reduce paid-in capital.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Investment expense 80,000; Dr Paid-in capital 40,000 / Cr Cash 120,000</p></blockquote><p><strong>Deferred consideration</strong>, a fixed amount payable later, is part of the consideration at fair value and a liability from the acquisition date. ACE's provisional consideration of 671,970 was 388,745 in cash at completion plus 283,225 deferred (Note 43, PDF p. 227).</p><p><strong>Contingent consideration</strong> depends on a future event, such as an earn-out. It enters the consideration at acquisition-date fair value (para. 39) and is classified as a liability or equity under PSAK 232 (para. 40). Later a liability is remeasured through profit or loss and an equity amount is not remeasured; neither touches goodwill after the measurement period (para. 58, restated in BRPT's policy, Note 3d, PDF p. 69). For example (illustration), an earn-out of 10,000 on two years' wind output is worth 2,800 at acquisition and 4,000 a year later.</p><blockquote><p>At acquisition: included in consideration, Cr Contingent consideration liability 2,800</p><p>One year later: Dr Loss on remeasurement of contingent consideration 1,200 / Cr Contingent consideration liability 1,200</p></blockquote><h2>5. Recognising and measuring what was acquired</h2><p>The acquirer recognises the identifiable assets and liabilities separately from goodwill (para. 10), at acquisition-date fair value (para. 18). Each must meet the definition of an asset or liability at that date, so a restructuring the buyer merely plans is not a liability (para. 11), and each must be part of the exchange, not a separate deal with the seller (para. 12).</p><p><strong>Items the seller never recorded are still recognised.</strong> An internally built brand, a customer list or a favourable contract is invisible in the seller's books but identifiable to a buyer (para. 13; see the Intangible Assets essay). A contingent liability is recognised if it is a present obligation whose fair value can be measured, even if an outflow is not probable, which PSAK 237 (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37) would not allow outside a combination (para. 23).</p><p><strong>Deferred tax and employee benefits follow their own standards.</strong> BRPT's policy names deferred tax under PSAK 212 (formerly PSAK 46, IAS 12) and employee benefits under PSAK 219 (formerly PSAK 24, IAS 19) (Note 3d, PDF p. 68).</p><p>In the Pam–Sun example (illustration) Sun's book net assets of 750,000 become 1,200,000 at fair value, including an unrecorded patent of 50,000 and notes payable revalued down to 135,000. An allocation entry that debits a single "Building – net" of 850,000, building 500,000 plus equipment 350,000, balances, but equipment needs its own line because the two have different lives.</p><h2>6. Goodwill and bargain purchase</h2><p>Goodwill is what the consideration buys beyond the identifiable net assets: synergies, the assembled workforce, expected new customers. It cannot be sold on its own, so it is measured as a residual (para. 32).</p><blockquote><p>Goodwill = consideration transferred + NCI + fair value of any previously held interest − identifiable net assets at fair value</p></blockquote><p>Two Pam–Sun cases (illustration), with identifiable net assets of 1,200,000:</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Case 1: goodwill</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Case 2: bargain purchase</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Consideration</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>400,000 cash + 50,000 shares × 20 = 1,400,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>40,000 shares × 20 + 200,000 bonds = 1,000,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Residual</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,400,000 − 1,200,000 = 200,000 goodwill</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,000,000 − 1,200,000 = 200,000 gain</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>A bargain purchase must survive a recheck.</strong> A negative residual often signals an error. Before recognising a gain the acquirer must reassess whether it has identified every asset and liability and review how it measured the net assets, any NCI, any previously held interest and the consideration (para. 36). Whatever remains is a gain in profit or loss on the acquisition date, attributed to the acquirer (para. 34). Forced sales and sellers leaving a business are the usual causes (para. 35). Case 2 above assumes the 10 percent bonds are worth their face value of 200,000, which holds only if 10 percent is the market rate.</p><p>Take ACE as the starting point (Note 43, PDF p. 227): identifiable net assets at fair value, after deferred tax, of 2,444,134 against a provisional consideration of 671,970.</p><blockquote><p>Gain = 2,444,134 − 671,970 = 1,772,164</p><p>Dr Cash 215,431; Dr Receivables 680,565; Dr Inventories 970,281; Dr Other current assets 6; Dr Right-of-use assets 307,351; Dr Fixed assets 1,604,141 / Cr Trade and other payables 563,778; Cr Other current liabilities 23,502; Cr Decommissioning and other provisions 121,519; Cr Lease liabilities 377,366; Cr Deferred tax liabilities 247,476; Cr Cash 388,745; Cr Deferred consideration 283,225; Cr Gain from bargain purchase 1,772,164</p></blockquote><p>BRPT attributes the gain to a different view of ACE's strategic direction between the seller and the group. For example (illustration), had the para. 36 review found an environmental obligation with a fair value of 50,000 that the valuation missed, the gain would fall by 50,000 before deferred tax, to 1,722,164.</p><h2>7. Purchase price allocation: from book value to goodwill</h2><p>On a stock acquisition the parent's ledger shows only the investment, so the fair values are rebuilt on the worksheet. An allocation schedule does this: price minus the subsidiary's book equity is the <em>excess</em>, which goes first to identifiable items whose fair value differs from book value, the remainder to goodwill.</p><p>Take as the starting point BRPT's acquisition of PT UPC Sidrap Bayu Energi (SBE), operator of a 75 MW wind farm with a 30-year PPA with PLN, the state electricity company, on 2 April 2024 (Note 43, PDF pp. 233–234). BRPT reports identifiable net assets of 38,459 before the PPA, a customer contract (the PPA) of 78,709, deferred tax liabilities on the step-ups of 20,941, goodwill of 12,051 and consideration of 108,278. Ownership of 99.99 percent is treated here as 100 percent. BRPT does not give SBE's book equity; assume 31,000 for illustration.</p><blockquote><p>Excess of price over book equity = 108,278 − 31,000 = 77,278</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 50px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Allocation of the excess</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Amount</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Fixed assets and other items, fair value over book (38,459 − 31,000)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>7,459</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Customer contract (PPA), unrecorded by SBE</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>78,709</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Deferred tax liability on the step-ups</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(20,941)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Goodwill, the remainder</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>12,051</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total excess</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>77,278</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Worksheet elimination at the acquisition date:</p><blockquote><p>Dr SBE share capital and retained earnings 31,000; Dr Fixed assets and other items 7,459; Dr Customer contract 78,709; Dr Goodwill 12,051 / Cr Deferred tax liability 20,941; Cr Investment in SBE 108,278</p></blockquote><p>Debits and credits are both 129,219; K2 amortises the PPA afterwards.</p><p><strong>Deferred tax can turn a gain into goodwill.</strong> Buying shares does not change the subsidiary's tax bases, so every fair value step-up is a taxable temporary difference (carrying amount above tax base, taxed as the asset is used), and PSAK 212 requires the deferred tax liability to be recognised in the acquisition accounting, which raises goodwill by the same amount (PSAK 103 para. 24 sends deferred tax to PSAK 212). For example (illustration), a step-up of 10,000 on turbines at a 22 percent tax rate:</p><blockquote><p>Deferred tax liability = 22% × 10,000 = 2,200; goodwill rises by 2,200</p><p>Each year the 1,000 of extra depreciation (ten-year life) releases 22% × 1,000 = 220: Dr Deferred tax liability 220 / Cr Deferred tax expense 220</p></blockquote><p>No deferred tax is recognised on goodwill itself (PSAK 212 para. 15(a)), because goodwill is the residual and a liability on it would raise goodwill again. At SBE, BREN's tax note puts 17,316 = 22% × 78,709 of the 20,941 on the contract and the rest on other step-ups (BREN FS 2025, PDF p. 121). Without the deferred tax line, identifiable net assets would be 38,459 + 78,709 = 117,168, above the price.</p><blockquote><p>117,168 − 108,278 = 8,890 bargain purchase gain, instead of goodwill of 12,051</p></blockquote><p><strong>With less than 100 percent, NCI enters the residual.</strong> In the Pam–Son case (illustration: 90 percent for 10,200,000; identifiable net assets at fair value 7,000,000), grossing the price up to an implied fair value of Son (10,200,000 ÷ 90%) of 11,333,333 gives NCI of 1,133,333 and goodwill of 4,333,333. PSAK 103 para. 19 allows NCI at fair value, but measured on the minority shares themselves, which carry no control premium, or at the proportionate share; the NCI essay works through the choice.</p><blockquote><p>Proportionate: NCI = 10% × 7,000,000 = 700,000; goodwill = 10,200,000 + 700,000 − 7,000,000 = 3,900,000</p></blockquote><p>An allocation entry for the Pam–Son case must include Dr Goodwill 4,333,333; its other lines debit only 1,400,000 against credits of 5,733,333.</p><h2>8. The measurement period</h2><p>Provisional amounts can be corrected against goodwill for up to one year, because valuing a refinery or a long-term contract takes months. Provisional amounts are adjusted retrospectively for new information about facts that existed at the acquisition date (para. 45). Adjustments go against goodwill or the bargain purchase gain (para. 48), and comparatives are revised as if the final figures had been booked at the acquisition date (para. 49). After the year, only errors are corrected (para. 50).</p><p>For example (illustration), BRPT paid 101,927 at completion, and a later verification required another 6,351 (Note 43, PDF p. 233). Suppose the 6,351 had been agreed eight months after closing, from completion accounts as at the acquisition date.</p><blockquote><p>Initial goodwill = 101,927 − (38,459 + 78,709 − 20,941) = 101,927 − 96,227 = 5,700</p><p>Adjustment within the measurement period: Dr Goodwill 6,351 / Cr Payable to sellers 6,351; final goodwill = 12,051</p></blockquote><p>BRPT's 2025 figures have two provisional elements. ACE's consideration "is provisional as the final consideration is subject to arbitration proceedings" (Note 43, PDF p. 227). APS's fair values and its gain of 95,875 are labelled provisional, and its purchase price allocation report is dated 23 March 2026 (PDF pp. 228–229).</p><h2>9. Push-down accounting</h2><p><em>Push-down accounting has no PSAK counterpart.</em> In it the subsidiary rewrites its own books to the parent's price, revaluing assets, recording goodwill and replacing retained earnings with "push-down capital". For example (illustration), Pop pays 5,000 shares at USD 55 plus 100,000 cash for Son.</p><blockquote><p>Price = 5,000 × 55 + 100,000 = 375,000; Son's identifiable net assets at fair value = 590,000 − 250,000 = 340,000; goodwill = 35,000</p></blockquote><p>Their balance sheet for Son prints "Goodwill $70.000" against total assets of 625,000, which fit 35,000; 70,000 is Son's land.</p><p>Under PSAK a subsidiary's own statements stay on its carrying amounts (the figures already in its books), because goodwill recorded there would be internally generated, which PSAK 238 forbids. PSAK 338's basis for conclusions uses the term differently, for carrying a parent's earlier purchase price allocation into a later common-control transfer. The 2012 text calls that conceptually right (DK19) but rules it out for common-control combinations as impracticable (DK22), and the 2025 revision moves towards it (section 10). The fair values therefore live only on the worksheet, with the same numbers:</p><blockquote><p>Dr Capital stock 150,000; Dr Retained earnings 110,000; Dr Inventories 20,000; Dr Land 40,000; Dr Equipment 40,000; Dr Goodwill 35,000 / Cr Building 20,000; Cr Investment in Son 375,000</p></blockquote><h2>10. Business combinations under common control</h2><p>When one party controls both sides before and after a transfer, no outsider has set the consideration, so fair values lose their anchor. PSAK 103 excludes these transfers (para. 2(c)), and Indonesia applies PSAK 338 <em>Kombinasi Bisnis Entitas Sepengendali</em> (formerly PSAK 38), set out with an acquisition-versus-pooling table in the Share Capital essay. The receiving entity pools: carrying amounts, no goodwill, the difference from the consideration in additional paid-in capital (APIC), never recycled, and comparatives restated (Note 3e, PDF pp. 70–71).</p><p><strong>Buying out a minority holder shows where the boundary sits.</strong> PSAK 338 para. 09 reads: "Transaksi pembelian saham atau aset neto milik pemegang saham nonpengendali (yang tidak berada dalam pengendalian yang sama dengan pemegang saham pengendali) merupakan transaksi yang mencakup perubahan substansi ekonomi kepemilikan dari pemegang saham nonpengendali ke pemegang saham pengendali. Oleh karena itu, transaksi ini bukan merupakan transaksi kombinasi bisnis entitas sepengendali." Only a minority holder outside the common control is excluded.</p><p>For example (illustration, sized on BRPT's 2025 purchase in section 12), a group company pays 200,000 for 51 percent minority stakes in two companies it already controls. The stakes are carried in the consolidated statements as NCI of 161,296.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Seller outside the common control (PSAK 110 para. 23)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Seller under the same controlling shareholder (PSAK 338)</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Goodwill</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>None</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>None</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Difference</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>200,000 − 161,296 = 38,704</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>38,704</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Entry</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr NCI 161,296; Dr Other equity component 38,704 / Cr Cash 200,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr NCI 161,296; Dr APIC 38,704 / Cr Cash 200,000</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Neither route touches profit; only the equity line differs. Had the group not controlled the companies before, PSAK 103 would apply instead, with fair values and goodwill.</p><p><strong>Carrying amounts carry old acquisitions with them.</strong> Goodwill that a transferred business recognised on an earlier third-party purchase moves across at its carrying amount (section 12 traces one case at BRPT). The 2025 revision of PSAK 338, effective 1 January 2026, switches to the transferring entity's carrying amounts rather than the transferred entity's, unless impracticable. BRPT's 2025 statements apply the 2012 text.</p><h2>11. Business combinations across industries</h2><p><strong>Renewable, property and shipping companies.</strong> Single-asset companies (a permitted project, a building, a vessel) bought through their shares mostly fail the definition of a business or pass the concentration test, so no goodwill or deferred tax on the step-up arises. BRPT applied the test to PT Marina Indah Maritim (MIM) in 2024, whose fair value was "concentrated in the value of the vessel" (Note 1d, PDF p. 42).</p><p><strong>Oil, gas and geothermal joint operations.</strong> Buying a participating interest in a joint operation that is a business is accounted for with the acquisition method's principles, including goodwill, under PSAK 111 (formerly PSAK 66, IFRS 11) para. 21A.</p><p><strong>Investment entities.</strong> A qualifying fund measures controlled investees at fair value through profit or loss, outside PSAK 103 (PSAK 110 para. 31).</p><h2>12. Presentation and analysis: Barito Pacific's Note 43</h2><p>BRPT's 2025 profit rests on a one-off gain from bargain purchase, most of which belongs to outside shareholders. Note 43 gives the PSAK 103 para. B64 disclosures, including the combined group's results as if acquired on 1 January (B64(q)) (PDF pp. 226–229).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>ACE</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>APS</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Acquisition date, interest</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1 April 2025, 100%</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1 August 2025, 100%</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Consideration</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>671,970 (provisional)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>13,076</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Identifiable net assets at fair value</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,444,134</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>108,951 (provisional)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Gain from bargain purchase</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,772,164</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>95,875 (provisional)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Revenue and loss since acquisition</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4,634,352; (337,222)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>104,838; (28,125)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Group revenue and profit if acquired 1 January</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>8,735,677; 1,589,142</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>7,846,975; 1,617,820</p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Total gain = 1,772,164 + 95,875 = 1,868,039</p><p>Profit before tax excluding the gain = 1,602,988 − 1,868,039 = (265,051)</p><p>Profit attributable to owners of the parent = 489,807 / 1,615,634 = 30.3% of profit for the year</p></blockquote><p>Without the gain the group shows a pre-tax loss, and ACE itself lost 337,222 in nine months. ACE sits under CAP, in which BRPT holds 34.63 percent directly and 3.92 percent indirectly, and under CAPGC, 80 percent owned within the CAP group (Note 1d, PDF p. 24). The gain is attributed to the acquirer, CAPGC, and flows up through each layer's NCI.</p><blockquote><p>BRPT's effective interest in ACE = (34.63% + 3.92% through its wholly owned Marigold) × 80% = 38.55% × 80% = 30.84%</p></blockquote><p>That is roughly consistent with the 30.3 percent of group profit attributed to BRPT's owners.</p><p><strong>Common control in BRPT's notes.</strong> On 1 October 2025 the group bought the 51 percent stakes of PT Buana Primatama Niaga (BPN) in PT Chandra Shipping International (CSI) and MIM, companies CAP already controlled (Note 1d, PDF pp. 41–42), and treated it as a common-control restructuring (Notes 29 and 31, PDF pp. 212, 216). BPN's "beneficial owner is the ultimate shareholder of CDI" (PT Chandra Daya Investasi Tbk, a CAP subsidiary; Note 45h, PDF p. 236), which fits para. 09.</p><blockquote><p>Consideration = 87,625 + 73,350 = 160,975; carrying amount acquired = 161,296; difference = 321, credited to APIC</p><p>APIC from common-control transactions: −641,745 + 321 = −641,424</p></blockquote><p>Pooling also explains part of BRPT's goodwill. BRPT took Star Energy Group Holdings at carrying amounts in 2018 (Note 1d, PDF p. 52), and the Salak and Darajat goodwill from Star Energy's purchase from Chevron sits at 485,814 in both BRPT's Note 21 (PDF p. 141) and BREN's statements (272,016 + 213,798; BREN FS 2025, PDF p. 91).</p><h2>13. Related essays</h2><ul><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/intangible-assets-net">intangible-assets-net</a>: identifiability and goodwill impairment.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/non-controlling-interest">non-controlling-interest</a>: NCI at acquisition and changes in ownership.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/share-capital">share-capital</a> and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/share-capital">additional-paid-in-capital</a>: PSAK 338 in full and the negative APIC it leaves.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets">deferred-tax-liabilities</a>: temporary differences on fair value step-ups.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/trade-payables">other-payables</a>: deferred consideration.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/consolidation/consolidation-worksheet-elimination-entries">K2</a>: amortising the excess after acquisition.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/consolidation/consolidated-cash-flows-and-mid-year-acquisitions">K5</a>: acquisitions in the cash flow statement and mid-year.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/consolidation/mutual-holdings-complex-group-structures">K6</a>: the BRPT, CAP and BREN layers.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>The CSI/MIM purchase fits PSAK 338's wording, since BPN's beneficial owner is CDI's ultimate shareholder (Note 45h), but three things stay open. The cash flow statement calls the 160,975 "additional investment in subsidiaries from non-controlling interest" (PDF p. 19). The paragraph sits in Note 31 <em>Other equity components</em> beside the NCI transactions. And the full 321 goes to BRPT's APIC although BRPT owns 38.55 percent of CAP, the buyer. That "ultimate shareholder of CDI" means BRPT's controlling shareholder is also read, not stated.</p></li><li><p>BRPT's SBE figures disagree within the FS. Note 43's table, BREN's statements (FY2024 PDF p. 82; FY2025 PDF pp. 77–78, 91) and BRPT's own SBE impairment test (Note 21, PDF p. 142: 85,020 = 78,709 − 5,740 + 12,051) use a customer contract of 78,709 and goodwill of 12,051. Note 43's text (12,333) and Note 21's cost roll-forward (customer contract 85,593 with different amortisation; goodwill addition 12,495 = 12,333 + OMI's 162) use other numbers. Section 7 uses the 78,709 and 12,051 set.</p></li><li><p>The book equity of 31,000 for SBE in section 7 is an assumption. BRPT does not disclose SBE's pre-acquisition carrying amounts, so the split of the excess between step-ups and goodwill is illustrative, though the fair values, goodwill and consideration are BRPT's.</p></li><li><p>Whether ACE's arbitration outcome will be a measurement-period adjustment depends on whether it reflects facts at 1 April 2025 and is settled by 1 April 2026; BRPT does not say. The 2026 interim statements were not checked.</p></li><li><p>That the Salak and Darajat goodwill entered BRPT at carrying amount under pooling is inferred from the identical 485,814 in BRPT's Note 21 and BREN's goodwill note; BRPT does not trace the goodwill through the 2018 combination.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Barito Pacific Tbk dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2025 and 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of profit or loss (PDF p. 17); statement of changes in equity (PDF p. 18); cash flow statement (PDF p. 19); Note 1d ownership table (PDF pp. 24, 28) and descriptions of CSI (PDF p. 41), MIM (PDF p. 42), ACE (PDF p. 43) and SEGHPL (PDF p. 52); Note 3d (PDF pp. 68–70); Note 3e (PDF pp. 70–71); Note 21 (PDF pp. 140–142); Note 22b (PDF p. 144); Note 29 (PDF p. 212); Note 31 (PDF p. 216); Note 43 (PDF pp. 226–234); Note 44 (PDF p. 235); Note 45h on BPN (PDF p. 236). BRPT FS. Page numbers computed from the text extraction and checked against the PDF; tables read from the raw text extraction where the two-column text was garbled. BREN FS 2025 (PDF pp. 77–78, 91, 121) and FY2024 (PDF p. 82) read for SBE's purchase price allocation, goodwill and deferred tax. ACE's 8 May 2024 announcement and 1 April 2025 completion: VERIFIED (web, ICIS, 14 November 2024; Reuters via New Straits Times, April 2025).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 103 <em>Kombinasi Bisnis</em> (IFRS 3): paras. 5, 8–14, 18–20, 22–23, 32, 34–40, 42, 45–50, 53 and B7A–B7B, B8, B12–B19 VERIFIED (web, AASB 3 text, standards.aasb.gov.au, "The acquisition method" and Appendix B). B64(q) VERIFIED (same source, via the independent recheck). Paras. 2(b), 24 and 58: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED, consistent with BRPT Note 3d. Para. 2(c) VERIFIED as cited in the Share Capital essay (IAI Discussion Paper PIR PSAK 338). Amendment on the definition of a business ratified by DSAK IAI on 31 July 2019, effective 1 January 2021: VERIFIED (web, IAI announcement via accounting.binus.ac.id; PwC Indonesia, <em>PSAK 2021</em>).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 338 <em>Kombinasi Bisnis Entitas Sepengendali</em> (formerly PSAK 38): para. 09 (quoted, with its parenthesis) and DK19–DK22 of the 2012 text, and DK23A onward of the 2025 revision, VERIFIED (web, IAI, DE PSAK 338 (Revisi 2025), which shows the 2012 text with tracked changes; para. 09 at p. 338.5, DK19–DK22 at pp. 338.28–29). Revision approved 29 October 2025, effective 1 January 2026: VERIFIED (web, IAI, <em>DSAK Terkini</em> vol. 7). Paras. 07–12 as recorded in the Share Capital essay.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 110 (IFRS 10) paras. 23, 31; PSAK 111 (IFRS 11) para. 21A; PSAK 212 (IAS 12) paras. 15(a) and 15(b); PSAK 219 (IAS 19); PSAK 232 (IAS 32); PSAK 236 (IAS 36); PSAK 237 (IAS 37); PSAK 238 (IAS 38); PSAK 109 (IFRS 9): numbers from the IAI renumbering rule, VERIFIED (web, IAI list) as in the FSLI series; content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Beams, Anthony, Bettinghaus and Smith, <em>Advanced Accounting</em>, chs. 1 and 3. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A business combination is a transaction in which an acquirer obtains control of a ","type":"text"},{"text":"business","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". Buying a set of assets that is not a business is an asset acquisition: the price is spread over the assets on relative fair values, and no goodwill or bargain purchase gain arises. The optional concentration test settles many cases quickly.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Under the acquisition method (PSAK 103) the acquirer measures the consideration and every identifiable asset and liability at acquisition-date fair value, including intangibles the seller never recorded. Goodwill is the residual. A negative residual is a bargain purchase gain in profit or loss, recognised only after the numbers have been rechecked. Provisional amounts may be corrected for up to one year.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When the same party controls both sides, nothing is bought from an outsider, so PSAK 338 replaces fair values with carrying amounts (pooling of interests) and puts the difference from the price in additional paid-in capital.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Barito Pacific Tbk and subsidiaries (BRPT) report a gain from bargain purchase of 1,868,039 for 2025, in USD thousand, against profit for the year of 1,615,634 (BRPT FS 2025, statement of profit or loss, PDF p. 17). Two purchases by its petrochemical subsidiary produced it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading this page a student should be able to classify a purchase, apply the acquisition method, allocate the excess of price over book value, handle the measurement period and record a common-control combination.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from BRPT's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. Why companies combine and the legal forms","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The legal form of a combination decides whose ledger the acquired assets land in, not how they are measured. The motives for combining range from cost savings to tax, and combinations are classified as horizontal (same line of business), vertical and conglomerate (unrelated lines). Vertical integration joins different stages of one supply chain, a refinery buying its crude supplier.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Form","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Formula","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"What the acquirer records in its own books","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Merger","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A + B = A","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"B's assets and liabilities directly; B is dissolved","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consolidation","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A + B = C","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"a new company C takes over both; A and B are dissolved","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Stock acquisition","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A controls B","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"an investment in B; B keeps its own books and is consolidated","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Measurement is the same for all three. In a merger the values go into A's ledger; in a stock acquisition they appear only on the consolidation worksheet (section 7). BRPT's 2025 purchases were stock acquisitions. CAPGC Pte. Ltd., a subsidiary of PT Chandra Asri Pacific Tbk (CAP), bought 100 percent of the shares of Aster Chemical and Energy Pte. Ltd. (ACE) from Shell Singapore (Note 1d, PDF p. 43).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. What is a business: combination or asset acquisition","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The acquisition method applies only when what is acquired is a business. PSAK 103 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Kombinasi Bisnis","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 22, IFRS 3) scopes out the acquisition of an asset or group of assets that is not a business (para. 2(b)) and combinations of entities under common control (para. 2(c)).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A business needs an input and a substantive process.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A business is an integrated set of activities and assets capable of being managed to provide goods or services to customers. Outputs are not required, but it needs at least an ","type":"text"},{"text":"input","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" and a ","type":"text"},{"text":"substantive process","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" that together significantly contribute to the ability to create outputs (para. B8). An operating wind farm with turbines, a power purchase agreement (PPA) and staff is a business; a development licence with no workforce is usually not.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The concentration test is a shortcut out of the definition.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Since 1 January 2021 an optional test, elected deal by deal (paras. B7A–B7B), applies. If substantially all of the fair value of the gross assets acquired is concentrated in a single identifiable asset or a group of similar assets, the set is not a business and no further analysis is needed. Cash, deferred tax assets and goodwill from deferred tax are left out of gross assets, and a tangible asset and an intangible asset are never \"similar\".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), take BRPT's acquisition of 51 percent of PT Lombok Timur Bayu Energi, a wind project company, on 3 January 2024 (Note 43, PDF pp. 231–232).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gross assets acquired = 1 cash + 44 receivables + 2 other current + 37 fixed assets + 7,700 project development costs = 7,784","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gross assets excluding cash = 7,783","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Concentration = 7,700 / 7,783 = 98.9%, substantially all in one asset","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The boundary changes five things.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Suppose (illustration) the buyer also paid 150 of legal fees and, had the project been a business, the development rights would have been valued independently at 6,078.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Asset acquisition","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Business combination","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Price allocation","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Spread over assets on relative fair values","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Each item at its own fair value","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill or bargain purchase gain","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Never","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Residual, here 3,122 + 49% × 4,500 − 4,500 = 827 goodwill (before deferred tax)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Transaction costs of 150","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Capitalised into the assets","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expensed (para. 53)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Deferred tax on book–tax differences","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Not recognised (initial recognition exemption, PSAK 212.15(b))","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Recognised, which changes goodwill","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Contingent payments","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"No settled rule; often recognised when the obligation arises","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fair value at acquisition date (section 4)","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In the business column, identifiable net assets are 6,122 − 7,700 + 6,078 = 4,500 and the non-controlling interest (NCI) at its proportionate share is 2,205. BRPT's actual entry follows the asset-acquisition column, with NCI of 49% × 6,122 = 3,000 (rounded) and no goodwill.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Project development costs 7,700; Dr Cash 1; Dr Receivables 44; Dr Other current assets 2; Dr Fixed assets 37 / Cr Accrued expenses 962; Cr Taxes payable 52; Cr Trade and other payables 648; Cr NCI 3,000; Cr Cash 3,122","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. The acquisition method: acquirer and acquisition date","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Control, not legal form or the signing date, fixes both the acquirer and the acquisition date, the first two of the method's four steps (para. 5).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The acquirer","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" is the entity that obtains control under PSAK 110 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Laporan Keuangan Konsolidasian","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10) (para. B13). When that is unclear, the standard looks at who pays, who issues shares, whose owners and managers dominate afterwards, and relative size (paras. B14–B17). In a ","type":"text"},{"text":"reverse acquisition","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", typical of a backdoor listing, the owners of a private company receive enough new shares of a listed shell to control it, so the private company is the accounting acquirer (para. B19).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The acquisition date","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" is the date control passes, usually the closing date, which may differ from the signing date (paras. 8–9). It fixes the fair values, the start of consolidation and the measurement period. Shell announced its agreement to sell to CAPGC on 8 May 2024, but control passed at completion on 1 April 2025, the acquisition date in Note 43 (PDF p. 226). Aster Polymer Solutions Pte. Ltd. (APS) follows the same pattern: agreement on 6 May 2025, effective and consolidated from 1 August 2025 (PDF pp. 228–229).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Consideration transferred and acquisition-related costs","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consideration","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":", the price the acquirer pays, is measured at fair value: cash paid, other assets given up, liabilities incurred to the former owners, and equity issued (para. 37). For example (illustration), Pop Corporation issues 100,000 shares of USD 10 par, market price USD 16, for the net assets of Son.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consideration = 100,000 × 16 = 1,600,000; common stock = 100,000 × 10 = 1,000,000; paid-in capital = 600,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Investment in Son 1,600,000 / Cr Common stock 1,000,000; Cr Paid-in capital 600,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Acquisition-related costs","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" are not part of the price. Advisory, legal, accounting, valuation and internal acquisition-department costs are expensed when incurred, and the costs of issuing debt or equity follow PSAK 232 and PSAK 109 (para. 53). In the Pop example the 80,000 consultant fee is expensed, and SEC fees, accountants' fees and share certificates (5,000 + 10,000 + 25,000 = 40,000) reduce paid-in capital.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Investment expense 80,000; Dr Paid-in capital 40,000 / Cr Cash 120,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Deferred consideration","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":", a fixed amount payable later, is part of the consideration at fair value and a liability from the acquisition date. ACE's provisional consideration of 671,970 was 388,745 in cash at completion plus 283,225 deferred (Note 43, PDF p. 227).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Contingent consideration","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" depends on a future event, such as an earn-out. It enters the consideration at acquisition-date fair value (para. 39) and is classified as a liability or equity under PSAK 232 (para. 40). Later a liability is remeasured through profit or loss and an equity amount is not remeasured; neither touches goodwill after the measurement period (para. 58, restated in BRPT's policy, Note 3d, PDF p. 69). For example (illustration), an earn-out of 10,000 on two years' wind output is worth 2,800 at acquisition and 4,000 a year later.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"At acquisition: included in consideration, Cr Contingent consideration liability 2,800","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"One year later: Dr Loss on remeasurement of contingent consideration 1,200 / Cr Contingent consideration liability 1,200","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Recognising and measuring what was acquired","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The acquirer recognises the identifiable assets and liabilities separately from goodwill (para. 10), at acquisition-date fair value (para. 18). Each must meet the definition of an asset or liability at that date, so a restructuring the buyer merely plans is not a liability (para. 11), and each must be part of the exchange, not a separate deal with the seller (para. 12).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Items the seller never recorded are still recognised.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" An internally built brand, a customer list or a favourable contract is invisible in the seller's books but identifiable to a buyer (para. 13; see the Intangible Assets essay). A contingent liability is recognised if it is a present obligation whose fair value can be measured, even if an outflow is not probable, which PSAK 237 (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37) would not allow outside a combination (para. 23).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Deferred tax and employee benefits follow their own standards.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" BRPT's policy names deferred tax under PSAK 212 (formerly PSAK 46, IAS 12) and employee benefits under PSAK 219 (formerly PSAK 24, IAS 19) (Note 3d, PDF p. 68).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In the Pam–Sun example (illustration) Sun's book net assets of 750,000 become 1,200,000 at fair value, including an unrecorded patent of 50,000 and notes payable revalued down to 135,000. An allocation entry that debits a single \"Building – net\" of 850,000, building 500,000 plus equipment 350,000, balances, but equipment needs its own line because the two have different lives.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Goodwill and bargain purchase","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill is what the consideration buys beyond the identifiable net assets: synergies, the assembled workforce, expected new customers. It cannot be sold on its own, so it is measured as a residual (para. 32).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill = consideration transferred + NCI + fair value of any previously held interest − identifiable net assets at fair value","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Two Pam–Sun cases (illustration), with identifiable net assets of 1,200,000:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Case 1: goodwill","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Case 2: bargain purchase","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consideration","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"400,000 cash + 50,000 shares × 20 = 1,400,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"40,000 shares × 20 + 200,000 bonds = 1,000,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Residual","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,400,000 − 1,200,000 = 200,000 goodwill","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,000,000 − 1,200,000 = 200,000 gain","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A bargain purchase must survive a recheck.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A negative residual often signals an error. Before recognising a gain the acquirer must reassess whether it has identified every asset and liability and review how it measured the net assets, any NCI, any previously held interest and the consideration (para. 36). Whatever remains is a gain in profit or loss on the acquisition date, attributed to the acquirer (para. 34). Forced sales and sellers leaving a business are the usual causes (para. 35). Case 2 above assumes the 10 percent bonds are worth their face value of 200,000, which holds only if 10 percent is the market rate.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Take ACE as the starting point (Note 43, PDF p. 227): identifiable net assets at fair value, after deferred tax, of 2,444,134 against a provisional consideration of 671,970.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gain = 2,444,134 − 671,970 = 1,772,164","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 215,431; Dr Receivables 680,565; Dr Inventories 970,281; Dr Other current assets 6; Dr Right-of-use assets 307,351; Dr Fixed assets 1,604,141 / Cr Trade and other payables 563,778; Cr Other current liabilities 23,502; Cr Decommissioning and other provisions 121,519; Cr Lease liabilities 377,366; Cr Deferred tax liabilities 247,476; Cr Cash 388,745; Cr Deferred consideration 283,225; Cr Gain from bargain purchase 1,772,164","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"BRPT attributes the gain to a different view of ACE's strategic direction between the seller and the group. For example (illustration), had the para. 36 review found an environmental obligation with a fair value of 50,000 that the valuation missed, the gain would fall by 50,000 before deferred tax, to 1,722,164.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Purchase price allocation: from book value to goodwill","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"On a stock acquisition the parent's ledger shows only the investment, so the fair values are rebuilt on the worksheet. An allocation schedule does this: price minus the subsidiary's book equity is the ","type":"text"},{"text":"excess","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", which goes first to identifiable items whose fair value differs from book value, the remainder to goodwill.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Take as the starting point BRPT's acquisition of PT UPC Sidrap Bayu Energi (SBE), operator of a 75 MW wind farm with a 30-year PPA with PLN, the state electricity company, on 2 April 2024 (Note 43, PDF pp. 233–234). BRPT reports identifiable net assets of 38,459 before the PPA, a customer contract (the PPA) of 78,709, deferred tax liabilities on the step-ups of 20,941, goodwill of 12,051 and consideration of 108,278. Ownership of 99.99 percent is treated here as 100 percent. BRPT does not give SBE's book equity; assume 31,000 for illustration.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Excess of price over book equity = 108,278 − 31,000 = 77,278","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Allocation of the excess","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Amount","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fixed assets and other items, fair value over book (38,459 − 31,000)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7,459","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Customer contract (PPA), unrecorded by SBE","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"78,709","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Deferred tax liability on the step-ups","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(20,941)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill, the remainder","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12,051","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total excess","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"77,278","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Worksheet elimination at the acquisition date:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr SBE share capital and retained earnings 31,000; Dr Fixed assets and other items 7,459; Dr Customer contract 78,709; Dr Goodwill 12,051 / Cr Deferred tax liability 20,941; Cr Investment in SBE 108,278","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Debits and credits are both 129,219; K2 amortises the PPA afterwards.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Deferred tax can turn a gain into goodwill.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Buying shares does not change the subsidiary's tax bases, so every fair value step-up is a taxable temporary difference (carrying amount above tax base, taxed as the asset is used), and PSAK 212 requires the deferred tax liability to be recognised in the acquisition accounting, which raises goodwill by the same amount (PSAK 103 para. 24 sends deferred tax to PSAK 212). For example (illustration), a step-up of 10,000 on turbines at a 22 percent tax rate:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Deferred tax liability = 22% × 10,000 = 2,200; goodwill rises by 2,200","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Each year the 1,000 of extra depreciation (ten-year life) releases 22% × 1,000 = 220: Dr Deferred tax liability 220 / Cr Deferred tax expense 220","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"No deferred tax is recognised on goodwill itself (PSAK 212 para. 15(a)), because goodwill is the residual and a liability on it would raise goodwill again. At SBE, BREN's tax note puts 17,316 = 22% × 78,709 of the 20,941 on the contract and the rest on other step-ups (BREN FS 2025, PDF p. 121). Without the deferred tax line, identifiable net assets would be 38,459 + 78,709 = 117,168, above the price.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"117,168 − 108,278 = 8,890 bargain purchase gain, instead of goodwill of 12,051","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"With less than 100 percent, NCI enters the residual.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" In the Pam–Son case (illustration: 90 percent for 10,200,000; identifiable net assets at fair value 7,000,000), grossing the price up to an implied fair value of Son (10,200,000 ÷ 90%) of 11,333,333 gives NCI of 1,133,333 and goodwill of 4,333,333. PSAK 103 para. 19 allows NCI at fair value, but measured on the minority shares themselves, which carry no control premium, or at the proportionate share; the NCI essay works through the choice.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Proportionate: NCI = 10% × 7,000,000 = 700,000; goodwill = 10,200,000 + 700,000 − 7,000,000 = 3,900,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An allocation entry for the Pam–Son case must include Dr Goodwill 4,333,333; its other lines debit only 1,400,000 against credits of 5,733,333.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. The measurement period","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Provisional amounts can be corrected against goodwill for up to one year, because valuing a refinery or a long-term contract takes months. Provisional amounts are adjusted retrospectively for new information about facts that existed at the acquisition date (para. 45). Adjustments go against goodwill or the bargain purchase gain (para. 48), and comparatives are revised as if the final figures had been booked at the acquisition date (para. 49). After the year, only errors are corrected (para. 50).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), BRPT paid 101,927 at completion, and a later verification required another 6,351 (Note 43, PDF p. 233). Suppose the 6,351 had been agreed eight months after closing, from completion accounts as at the acquisition date.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Initial goodwill = 101,927 − (38,459 + 78,709 − 20,941) = 101,927 − 96,227 = 5,700","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Adjustment within the measurement period: Dr Goodwill 6,351 / Cr Payable to sellers 6,351; final goodwill = 12,051","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"BRPT's 2025 figures have two provisional elements. ACE's consideration \"is provisional as the final consideration is subject to arbitration proceedings\" (Note 43, PDF p. 227). APS's fair values and its gain of 95,875 are labelled provisional, and its purchase price allocation report is dated 23 March 2026 (PDF pp. 228–229).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Push-down accounting","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Push-down accounting has no PSAK counterpart.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" In it the subsidiary rewrites its own books to the parent's price, revaluing assets, recording goodwill and replacing retained earnings with \"push-down capital\". For example (illustration), Pop pays 5,000 shares at USD 55 plus 100,000 cash for Son.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Price = 5,000 × 55 + 100,000 = 375,000; Son's identifiable net assets at fair value = 590,000 − 250,000 = 340,000; goodwill = 35,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Their balance sheet for Son prints \"Goodwill $70.000\" against total assets of 625,000, which fit 35,000; 70,000 is Son's land.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Under PSAK a subsidiary's own statements stay on its carrying amounts (the figures already in its books), because goodwill recorded there would be internally generated, which PSAK 238 forbids. PSAK 338's basis for conclusions uses the term differently, for carrying a parent's earlier purchase price allocation into a later common-control transfer. The 2012 text calls that conceptually right (DK19) but rules it out for common-control combinations as impracticable (DK22), and the 2025 revision moves towards it (section 10). The fair values therefore live only on the worksheet, with the same numbers:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Capital stock 150,000; Dr Retained earnings 110,000; Dr Inventories 20,000; Dr Land 40,000; Dr Equipment 40,000; Dr Goodwill 35,000 / Cr Building 20,000; Cr Investment in Son 375,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Business combinations under common control","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When one party controls both sides before and after a transfer, no outsider has set the consideration, so fair values lose their anchor. PSAK 103 excludes these transfers (para. 2(c)), and Indonesia applies PSAK 338 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Kombinasi Bisnis Entitas Sepengendali","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 38), set out with an acquisition-versus-pooling table in the Share Capital essay. The receiving entity pools: carrying amounts, no goodwill, the difference from the consideration in additional paid-in capital (APIC), never recycled, and comparatives restated (Note 3e, PDF pp. 70–71).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Buying out a minority holder shows where the boundary sits.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PSAK 338 para. 09 reads: \"Transaksi pembelian saham atau aset neto milik pemegang saham nonpengendali (yang tidak berada dalam pengendalian yang sama dengan pemegang saham pengendali) merupakan transaksi yang mencakup perubahan substansi ekonomi kepemilikan dari pemegang saham nonpengendali ke pemegang saham pengendali. Oleh karena itu, transaksi ini bukan merupakan transaksi kombinasi bisnis entitas sepengendali.\" Only a minority holder outside the common control is excluded.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration, sized on BRPT's 2025 purchase in section 12), a group company pays 200,000 for 51 percent minority stakes in two companies it already controls. The stakes are carried in the consolidated statements as NCI of 161,296.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Seller outside the common control (PSAK 110 para. 23)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Seller under the same controlling shareholder (PSAK 338)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"None","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"None","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Difference","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"200,000 − 161,296 = 38,704","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"38,704","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Entry","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr NCI 161,296; Dr Other equity component 38,704 / Cr Cash 200,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr NCI 161,296; Dr APIC 38,704 / Cr Cash 200,000","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Neither route touches profit; only the equity line differs. Had the group not controlled the companies before, PSAK 103 would apply instead, with fair values and goodwill.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Carrying amounts carry old acquisitions with them.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Goodwill that a transferred business recognised on an earlier third-party purchase moves across at its carrying amount (section 12 traces one case at BRPT). The 2025 revision of PSAK 338, effective 1 January 2026, switches to the transferring entity's carrying amounts rather than the transferred entity's, unless impracticable. BRPT's 2025 statements apply the 2012 text.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. Business combinations across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Renewable, property and shipping companies.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Single-asset companies (a permitted project, a building, a vessel) bought through their shares mostly fail the definition of a business or pass the concentration test, so no goodwill or deferred tax on the step-up arises. BRPT applied the test to PT Marina Indah Maritim (MIM) in 2024, whose fair value was \"concentrated in the value of the vessel\" (Note 1d, PDF p. 42).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Oil, gas and geothermal joint operations.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Buying a participating interest in a joint operation that is a business is accounted for with the acquisition method's principles, including goodwill, under PSAK 111 (formerly PSAK 66, IFRS 11) para. 21A.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Investment entities.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A qualifying fund measures controlled investees at fair value through profit or loss, outside PSAK 103 (PSAK 110 para. 31).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12. Presentation and analysis: Barito Pacific's Note 43","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"BRPT's 2025 profit rests on a one-off gain from bargain purchase, most of which belongs to outside shareholders. Note 43 gives the PSAK 103 para. B64 disclosures, including the combined group's results as if acquired on 1 January (B64(q)) (PDF pp. 226–229).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"ACE","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"APS","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Acquisition date, interest","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1 April 2025, 100%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1 August 2025, 100%","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consideration","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"671,970 (provisional)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"13,076","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Identifiable net assets at fair value","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,444,134","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"108,951 (provisional)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gain from bargain purchase","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,772,164","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"95,875 (provisional)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Revenue and loss since acquisition","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,634,352; (337,222)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"104,838; (28,125)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Group revenue and profit if acquired 1 January","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8,735,677; 1,589,142","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7,846,975; 1,617,820","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total gain = 1,772,164 + 95,875 = 1,868,039","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Profit before tax excluding the gain = 1,602,988 − 1,868,039 = (265,051)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Profit attributable to owners of the parent = 489,807 / 1,615,634 = 30.3% of profit for the year","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Without the gain the group shows a pre-tax loss, and ACE itself lost 337,222 in nine months. ACE sits under CAP, in which BRPT holds 34.63 percent directly and 3.92 percent indirectly, and under CAPGC, 80 percent owned within the CAP group (Note 1d, PDF p. 24). The gain is attributed to the acquirer, CAPGC, and flows up through each layer's NCI.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"BRPT's effective interest in ACE = (34.63% + 3.92% through its wholly owned Marigold) × 80% = 38.55% × 80% = 30.84%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That is roughly consistent with the 30.3 percent of group profit attributed to BRPT's owners.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Common control in BRPT's notes.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" On 1 October 2025 the group bought the 51 percent stakes of PT Buana Primatama Niaga (BPN) in PT Chandra Shipping International (CSI) and MIM, companies CAP already controlled (Note 1d, PDF pp. 41–42), and treated it as a common-control restructuring (Notes 29 and 31, PDF pp. 212, 216). BPN's \"beneficial owner is the ultimate shareholder of CDI\" (PT Chandra Daya Investasi Tbk, a CAP subsidiary; Note 45h, PDF p. 236), which fits para. 09.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consideration = 87,625 + 73,350 = 160,975; carrying amount acquired = 161,296; difference = 321, credited to APIC","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"APIC from common-control transactions: −641,745 + 321 = −641,424","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Pooling also explains part of BRPT's goodwill. BRPT took Star Energy Group Holdings at carrying amounts in 2018 (Note 1d, PDF p. 52), and the Salak and Darajat goodwill from Star Energy's purchase from Chevron sits at 485,814 in both BRPT's Note 21 (PDF p. 141) and BREN's statements (272,016 + 213,798; BREN FS 2025, PDF p. 91).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"13. Related essays","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"intangible-assets-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/intangible-assets-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": identifiability and goodwill impairment.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"non-controlling-interest","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/non-controlling-interest","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": NCI at acquisition and changes in ownership.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"share-capital","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/share-capital","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"text":"additional-paid-in-capital","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/share-capital","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": PSAK 338 in full and the negative APIC it leaves.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"deferred-tax-liabilities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": temporary differences on fair value step-ups.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-payables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/trade-payables","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": deferred consideration.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"K2","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/consolidation/consolidation-worksheet-elimination-entries","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": amortising the excess after acquisition.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"K5","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/consolidation/consolidated-cash-flows-and-mid-year-acquisitions","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": acquisitions in the cash flow statement and mid-year.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"K6","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/consolidation/mutual-holdings-complex-group-structures","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the BRPT, CAP and BREN layers.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The CSI/MIM purchase fits PSAK 338's wording, since BPN's beneficial owner is CDI's ultimate shareholder (Note 45h), but three things stay open. The cash flow statement calls the 160,975 \"additional investment in subsidiaries from non-controlling interest\" (PDF p. 19). The paragraph sits in Note 31 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Other equity components","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" beside the NCI transactions. And the full 321 goes to BRPT's APIC although BRPT owns 38.55 percent of CAP, the buyer. That \"ultimate shareholder of CDI\" means BRPT's controlling shareholder is also read, not stated.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"BRPT's SBE figures disagree within the FS. Note 43's table, BREN's statements (FY2024 PDF p. 82; FY2025 PDF pp. 77–78, 91) and BRPT's own SBE impairment test (Note 21, PDF p. 142: 85,020 = 78,709 − 5,740 + 12,051) use a customer contract of 78,709 and goodwill of 12,051. Note 43's text (12,333) and Note 21's cost roll-forward (customer contract 85,593 with different amortisation; goodwill addition 12,495 = 12,333 + OMI's 162) use other numbers. Section 7 uses the 78,709 and 12,051 set.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The book equity of 31,000 for SBE in section 7 is an assumption. BRPT does not disclose SBE's pre-acquisition carrying amounts, so the split of the excess between step-ups and goodwill is illustrative, though the fair values, goodwill and consideration are BRPT's.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Whether ACE's arbitration outcome will be a measurement-period adjustment depends on whether it reflects facts at 1 April 2025 and is settled by 1 April 2026; BRPT does not say. The 2026 interim statements were not checked.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That the Salak and Darajat goodwill entered BRPT at carrying amount under pooling is inferred from the identical 485,814 in BRPT's Note 21 and BREN's goodwill note; BRPT does not trace the goodwill through the 2018 combination.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Barito Pacific Tbk dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2025 and 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of profit or loss (PDF p. 17); statement of changes in equity (PDF p. 18); cash flow statement (PDF p. 19); Note 1d ownership table (PDF pp. 24, 28) and descriptions of CSI (PDF p. 41), MIM (PDF p. 42), ACE (PDF p. 43) and SEGHPL (PDF p. 52); Note 3d (PDF pp. 68–70); Note 3e (PDF pp. 70–71); Note 21 (PDF pp. 140–142); Note 22b (PDF p. 144); Note 29 (PDF p. 212); Note 31 (PDF p. 216); Note 43 (PDF pp. 226–234); Note 44 (PDF p. 235); Note 45h on BPN (PDF p. 236). BRPT FS. Page numbers computed from the text extraction and checked against the PDF; tables read from the raw text extraction where the two-column text was garbled. BREN FS 2025 (PDF pp. 77–78, 91, 121) and FY2024 (PDF p. 82) read for SBE's purchase price allocation, goodwill and deferred tax. ACE's 8 May 2024 announcement and 1 April 2025 completion: VERIFIED (web, ICIS, 14 November 2024; Reuters via New Straits Times, April 2025).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 103 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Kombinasi Bisnis","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IFRS 3): paras. 5, 8–14, 18–20, 22–23, 32, 34–40, 42, 45–50, 53 and B7A–B7B, B8, B12–B19 VERIFIED (web, AASB 3 text, standards.aasb.gov.au, \"The acquisition method\" and Appendix B). B64(q) VERIFIED (same source, via the independent recheck). Paras. 2(b), 24 and 58: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED, consistent with BRPT Note 3d. Para. 2(c) VERIFIED as cited in the Share Capital essay (IAI Discussion Paper PIR PSAK 338). Amendment on the definition of a business ratified by DSAK IAI on 31 July 2019, effective 1 January 2021: VERIFIED (web, IAI announcement via accounting.binus.ac.id; PwC Indonesia, ","type":"text"},{"text":"PSAK 2021","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":").","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 338 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Kombinasi Bisnis Entitas Sepengendali","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 38): para. 09 (quoted, with its parenthesis) and DK19–DK22 of the 2012 text, and DK23A onward of the 2025 revision, VERIFIED (web, IAI, DE PSAK 338 (Revisi 2025), which shows the 2012 text with tracked changes; para. 09 at p. 338.5, DK19–DK22 at pp. 338.28–29). Revision approved 29 October 2025, effective 1 January 2026: VERIFIED (web, IAI, ","type":"text"},{"text":"DSAK Terkini","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" vol. 7). Paras. 07–12 as recorded in the Share Capital essay.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 110 (IFRS 10) paras. 23, 31; PSAK 111 (IFRS 11) para. 21A; PSAK 212 (IAS 12) paras. 15(a) and 15(b); PSAK 219 (IAS 19); PSAK 232 (IAS 32); PSAK 236 (IAS 36); PSAK 237 (IAS 37); PSAK 238 (IAS 38); PSAK 109 (IFRS 9): numbers from the IAI renumbering rule, VERIFIED (web, IAI list) as in the FSLI series; content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Beams, Anthony, Bettinghaus and Smith, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Advanced Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", chs. 1 and 3. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of accounting for an acquisition from the definition of a business to pooling of interests, worked from Barito Pacific's 2025 purchases and set beside the industries where the boundary matters most","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"related_fsli":["intangible-assets-net","non-controlling-interest","share-capital","deferred-tax-assets","trade-payables"],"key_takeaways":["A business combination is a transaction in which an acquirer obtains control of a business. Buying a set of assets that is not a business is an asset acquisition: the price is spread over the assets on relative fair values, and no goodwill or bargain purchase gain arises. The optional concentration test settles many cases quickly.","Under the acquisition method (PSAK 103) the acquirer measures the consideration and every identifiable asset and liability at acquisition-date fair value, including intangibles the seller never recorded. Goodwill is the residual. A negative residual is a bargain purchase gain in profit or loss, recognised only after the numbers have been rechecked. Provisional amounts may be corrected for up to one year.","When the same party controls both sides, nothing is bought from an outsider, so PSAK 338 replaces fair values with carrying amounts (pooling of interests) and puts the difference from the price in additional paid-in capital."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = '9d9e015d-22ec-409b-863c-788653d84d50' AND updated_at = '2026-10-12T09:47:08.318531+00:00'::timestamptz;
