-- cash-and-cash-equivalents-definition-control-and-flows
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>Cash is money the entity can spend today, meaning cash on hand and demand deposits. A cash equivalent is an investment held for short-term cash needs that converts to a known amount with insignificant risk, which in practice means a maturity of three months or less from the date it was acquired. Everything else that looks like cash, from post-dated cheques to six-month deposits, belongs on another line.</p></li><li><p>Cash is the asset most exposed to error and theft, so its accounting is mostly control, through an imprest petty cash fund and a bank reconciliation that separates items the bank has not yet recorded from items the books have not yet recorded. Only the second kind needs a journal.</p></li><li><p>The balance sheet shows where cash ended up; the cash flow statement (PSAK 207) explains how it got there, through operating, investing and financing flows plus a separate line for exchange-rate effects. Moves between cash and cash equivalents are not cash flows, and an overdraft that is part of day-to-day cash management can count as negative cash in the flow statement while staying a liability on the balance sheet.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report cash and cash equivalents of 2,614,318 at 31 December 2024 against 5,018,739 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; "Daftar" is the printed page). The line is 8.6 percent of total assets and 32.5 percent of current assets, down from 16.2 and 50.8 percent. This page uses those balances as the starting point for its examples.</p><p>After reading it a student should be able to say what belongs in the line, record the entries that keep it accurate, and trace a change in cash through the cash flow statement.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. The order follows the chapter on cash in Kieso's <em>Intermediate Accounting</em> (IFRS edition) and the structure of PSAK 207.</p><h2>1. What counts as cash</h2><p>Cash is the one asset whose value needs no estimate, and the definition protects that. PSAK 207 <em>Statement of Cash Flows</em> (formerly PSAK 2, IAS 7) defines <em>cash</em> as cash on hand and demand deposits (para. 6). A <em>demand deposit</em> is a bank balance the holder can withdraw at any time without notice or penalty, such as a current account (<em>giro</em>) or a savings account.</p><p>The test is whether the item is money now. Kieso's textbook works through the items a cashier finds in the drawer, and they sort into three groups.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Item</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Where it goes</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Why</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Coins, banknotes, cheques and money orders received but not yet deposited</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>The bank will accept them as money at once</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Current accounts and savings accounts</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Withdrawable on demand</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Petty cash and change funds</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Money on hand, kept for small payments</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Post-dated cheques from customers</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Trade receivables</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Not payable until a later date</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cheques returned unpaid (NSF, "not sufficient funds")</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Receivables</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>A claim on the customer, not money</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Employee IOUs and travel advances</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Other receivables or prepayments</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Money will come back as cash or as receipts for expenses</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Postage stamps, prepaid vouchers</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Prepaid expenses</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Already spent on a future service</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Time deposits</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash equivalents or investments</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Depends on maturity at acquisition (section 2)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Overdrawn bank accounts</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Liabilities</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Money owed to the bank (section 4)</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>PHE's Note 6 has three components, cash on hand of 171, cash in banks of 2,476,825 and time deposits of 137,323 (Daftar 5/90, PDF p. 107). For a group with more than 2.6 billion dollars of cash, 171 of notes and coins is almost nothing, the pattern of a business that settles almost everything through banks.</p><h2>2. Cash equivalents and the three-month rule</h2><p>A cash equivalent is defined by its purpose and by the certainty of what it converts into. PSAK 207 calls cash equivalents "short-term, highly liquid investments that are readily convertible to known amounts of cash and which are subject to an insignificant risk of changes in value" (para. 6), held "for the purpose of meeting short-term cash commitments rather than for investment or other purposes" (para. 7). Two consequences follow.</p><ul><li><p><em>Equity investments are excluded</em> unless they are cash equivalents in substance, such as preference shares bought shortly before a fixed redemption date (para. 7). A listed share can be sold in a minute, but nobody knows for how much.</p></li><li><p><em>Maturity is measured from the date of acquisition.</em> The standard says an investment "normally" qualifies only with a maturity of "three months or less from the date of acquisition" (para. 7). A six-month deposit therefore never becomes a cash equivalent, even when only one month is left. A two-year government bond bought with two months to run, for cash management, can qualify from the day it is bought.</p></li></ul><p>PHE's policy uses the same boundary, admitting time deposits "with maturity periods of three months or less at the time of placement which are not used as collateral or are not restricted" (Note 3f, Daftar 5/38, PDF p. 55).</p><p>For example (illustration), on 1 October 2024 a PHE subsidiary has 100,000 more than it needs for the next quarter. Its treasury places 40,000 in a three-month USD deposit maturing on 1 January 2025 and 60,000 in a six-month deposit maturing on 1 April 2025, both at 5.0 percent a year.</p><blockquote><p>Three-month deposit: maturity at placement = 3 months, so it is a cash equivalent</p><p>Six-month deposit: maturity at placement = 6 months, so it is a short-term investment</p><p>Interest accrued at 31 December = 40,000 × 5.0% × 3/12 = 500 (three-month); 60,000 × 5.0% × 3/12 = 750 (six-month)</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Three-month deposit</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Six-month deposit</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1 October</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash equivalents, time deposits 40,000 / Cr Cash in banks 40,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Short-term investments, time deposits 60,000 / Cr Cash in banks 60,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 December</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Interest receivable 500 / Cr Finance income 500</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Interest receivable 750 / Cr Finance income 750</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash flow statement, 2024</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No flow: a move within cash and cash equivalents (para. 9)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Investing outflow of 60,000</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both deposits are measured at <em>amortised cost</em>, principal plus interest accrued at the deposit's own rate; here the accrued interest is shown as a receivable, though some entities add it to the deposit. They differ only in the line they sit on, and that decides whether the placement is a cash flow at all.</p><h2>3. Restricted cash and compensating balances</h2><p>A demand deposit stays cash even when a contract says what it may be spent on; it leaves the line only when the entity cannot withdraw it on demand. The IFRS Interpretations Committee concluded in April 2022 that restrictions on use agreed with a third party do not stop a deposit being cash "unless those restrictions change the nature of the deposit in a way that it would no longer meet the definition of cash". A restriction that leaves the money withdrawable is handled by disclosure.</p><p>A balance the entity cannot withdraw, because a bank blocks it as collateral or because a third party such as a regulator or a lender must consent to each withdrawal, is <em>restricted cash</em>. Kieso's <em>compensating balance</em>, a minimum deposit a lender requires as part of a loan, is the textbook case when the agreement legally blocks it. PHE shows restricted cash on two separate lines, current 154,217 and non-current 1,890,493 (Note 7, Daftar 5/93, PDF p. 110), and the companion page <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/restricted-cash">restricted-cash</a> treats it in full.</p><h2>4. Bank overdrafts and offsetting</h2><p>An overdraft is a loan from the bank, so it is a liability. Two different rules then decide whether it may be netted against positive balances, one for the balance sheet and one for the cash flow statement.</p><p>On the balance sheet, PSAK 232 <em>Financial Instruments: Presentation</em> (formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32) allows a financial asset and a financial liability to be shown net only when the entity has a legally enforceable right to set them off and intends to settle net or simultaneously (para. 42). Accounts at different banks almost never meet the test. Accounts at the same bank meet it only if the agreement gives a set-off right and the entity intends to settle net.</p><p>In the cash flow statement, PSAK 207 makes an exception to the rule that bank borrowings are financing. Overdrafts "repayable on demand" that "form an integral part of an entity's cash management" are a negative component of cash and cash equivalents, typically where "the bank balance often fluctuates from being positive to overdrawn" (para. 8).</p><p>For example (illustration), at 31 December 2024 a PHE subsidiary has 5,000 in its account at bank X and an operating account at bank Y overdrawn by 1,200. The account at Y swings between positive and overdrawn as invoices fall due before monthly sales receipts arrive, and bank Y can call the overdraft at any time.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Balance sheet</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash flow statement</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Rule</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 232.42: no right of set-off between banks X and Y</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 207.8: overdraft repayable on demand and part of cash management</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash and cash equivalents</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>5,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>5,000 − 1,200 = 3,800</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Overdraft</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,200 within short-term bank loans</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Not a financing flow; drawing and repaying it are movements within cash</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The note on cash must then reconcile the 3,800 in the cash flow statement with the 5,000 on the balance sheet (para. 45). PHE's short-term bank loans of 15,775, part of which PDSI draws through a notional pooling arrangement, are shown as loans (Note 26, Daftar 5/125, PDF p. 142); its cash flow statement closes at the same 2,614,318 as the balance sheet, so no overdraft is netted into cash.</p><h2>5. Controlling cash: petty cash</h2><p>Small payments are made from a fund kept on hand, and the <em>imprest system</em> keeps it under control by fixing its size. A custodian holds a set amount. Each payment is supported by a receipt, so receipts plus remaining cash should always equal the fixed amount. When the fund runs low it is topped back up to that amount, and the expenses are recorded only then, from the receipts.</p><p>For example (illustration), a PHE field office sets up a petty cash fund of 8.0 (USD 8 thousand). By month end the custodian holds receipts for local transport 3.1, meals for a site crew 2.4 and office supplies 1.6, and counts 0.8 of cash.</p><blockquote><p>Receipts + cash = 3.1 + 2.4 + 1.6 + 0.8 = 7.9, against a fund of 8.0, so 0.1 is short</p><p>Replenishment = 8.0 − 0.8 = 7.2</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 50px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Establish the fund</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Replenish at month end</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Petty cash 8.0</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Transport expense 3.1, Dr Meals expense 2.4, Dr Office supplies expense 1.6, Dr Cash over and short 0.1</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cr Cash in banks 8.0</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cr Cash in banks 7.2</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The petty cash account moves only when the fund's fixed size is changed. Setting up and replenishing the fund move money between two cash accounts, so neither is a cash flow; the outflow is what the custodian paid out. <em>Cash over and short</em> is an expense when short and other income when over, and a shortage that recurs is a control finding. Kieso's other controls follow the same logic: separate handling cash from recording it, deposit receipts intact daily, and pay everything else through the bank.</p><h2>6. Controlling cash: the bank reconciliation</h2><p>The bank's record and the entity's record of the same account rarely agree at month end, and the <em>bank reconciliation</em> explains why. Each side is adjusted for what it does not yet know, until both reach the same correct balance.</p><blockquote><p>Bank side: balance per bank + deposits in transit − outstanding cheques ± bank errors = correct balance</p><p>Book side: balance per books + collections and interest credited by the bank − bank charges − returned (NSF) cheques ± book errors = correct balance</p></blockquote><p>For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary's USD account at 31 December 2024.</p><ul><li><p>The bank statement shows 12,480. A deposit of 1,350 made on 31 December reaches the bank in January (deposit in transit). Cheques of 2,940 issued to vendors have not been presented (outstanding cheques).</p></li><li><p>The books show 9,912. The bank has credited interest of 62 and a joint-venture partner's cash call of 800 paid directly into the account, and has deducted charges of 4. A cheque for 150 from a scrap buyer came back unpaid. A vendor payment of 1,250 was recorded in the books as 1,520.</p></li></ul><blockquote><p>Bank side = 12,480 + 1,350 − 2,940 = 10,890</p><p>Book side = 9,912 + 62 + 800 − 4 − 150 + (1,520 − 1,250) = 9,912 + 62 + 800 − 4 − 150 + 270 = 10,890</p></blockquote><p>Both sides reach 10,890, which is the cash the balance sheet should show. Bank-side items need no entry, because the books are already right about them; the bank will catch up in January. Book-side items each need a journal.</p><table style="min-width: 50px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Item</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Journal</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest credited</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash in banks 62 / Cr Finance income 62</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Partner's cash call received</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash in banks 800 / Cr Receivable from joint operation partners 800</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Bank charges</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Bank charges expense 4 / Cr Cash in banks 4</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Returned cheque</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Other receivables, scrap buyer 150 / Cr Cash in banks 150</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Recording error</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash in banks 270 / Cr Trade payables 270</p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Book balance after the five entries = 9,912 + 62 + 800 − 4 − 150 + 270 = 10,890</p></blockquote><p>The recording error is credited to trade payables because the original entry debited payables with 1,520 when only 1,250 was settled.</p><h2>7. Foreign currency cash and credit losses on bank balances</h2><p>Cash in another currency is a <em>monetary item</em>, an amount fixed in units of that currency, so its value in the functional currency moves with the exchange rate. PSAK 221 <em>The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates</em> (formerly PSAK 10, IAS 21) retranslates monetary items at the closing rate at each reporting date and takes the difference to profit or loss. PHE's functional currency is the US dollar for every entity except Elnusa, and its monetary items are translated at the closing JISDOR rate, Bank Indonesia's reference rate (Note 3r, Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83). PHE held rupiah bank accounts of 375,921 at the end of 2024 (Note 6, Daftar 5/91, PDF p. 108).</p><p>For example (illustration), a subsidiary holds IDR 154,000 million throughout 2024 to pay local vendors, while the rupiah weakens from 15,400 to 16,000 per dollar (constructed rates).</p><blockquote><p>USD value at 1 January = IDR 154,000 million / 15,400 = USD 10.0 million = 10,000</p><p>USD value at 31 December = IDR 154,000 million / 16,000 = 9,625</p><p>Exchange loss = 10,000 − 9,625 = 375</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Dr Foreign exchange loss 375 / Cr Cash in banks 375</p></blockquote><p>No money left the account, so the 375 is not a cash flow. PSAK 207 still shows it, on a separate line after the three activities, so that opening cash plus flows plus the exchange effect equals closing cash (para. 28). If flows had occurred during the year, that line would also absorb the difference between translating them at transaction-date rates and at the closing rate (paras. 25, 28).</p><p>Bank balances are also financial assets at amortised cost, so PSAK 109 <em>Financial Instruments</em> (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9) requires an <em>expected credit loss</em> (ECL), the probability-weighted loss if the bank fails. For a bank with low credit risk the entity may assume credit risk has not increased significantly and measure only twelve-month expected losses. For example (illustration), 1,000,000 at a domestic bank with a 0.05 percent one-year probability of default and a 45 percent loss if default occurs.</p><blockquote><p>ECL = 1,000,000 × 0.05% × 45% = 225</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Dr Impairment loss on financial assets 225 / Cr Allowance for credit losses, cash in banks 225</p></blockquote><p>The amount is usually immaterial, which is why cash notes seldom show it. PHE reports that 4,182,445 of its 4,521,534 of cash in banks and restricted cash sits with Pefindo-rated counterparties, 338,966 with Fitch, Moody's or S&amp;P-rated ones and 123 with unrated ones (Note 49c, Daftar 5/232, PDF p. 249).</p><h2>8. From the balance to the cash flow statement</h2><p>The cash flow statement exists because two balances cannot explain the change between them. PSAK 207 sorts every cash flow into three activities (para. 6).</p><ul><li><p><em>Operating</em>: the principal revenue-producing activities, and anything that is not investing or financing.</p></li><li><p><em>Investing</em>: the acquisition and disposal of long-term assets and other investments not included in cash equivalents. Only spending that results in a recognised asset may be classified here (para. 16).</p></li><li><p><em>Financing</em>: changes in equity and borrowings.</p></li></ul><p>Movements between items that are all cash or cash equivalents are not flows (para. 9). Operating cash flows can be shown by the <em>direct method</em>, which lists gross receipts and payments, or the <em>indirect method</em>, which starts from profit and adjusts for non-cash items and working capital. The standard encourages the direct method (para. 19), and PHE uses it (Note 3a, Daftar 5/29, PDF p. 46).</p><p>The examples above land in the statement as follows.</p><table style="min-width: 50px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Item from this page</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash flow treatment</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Three-month deposit (section 2)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No flow</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Six-month deposit (section 2)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Investing outflow 60,000, and an investing inflow on maturity</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Overdraft at bank Y (section 4)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Negative cash; no financing flow</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Petty cash spending (section 5)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Operating outflow 7.2 (payments 7.1 and the 0.1 shortage), recorded at replenishment; setting up and topping up the fund are moves within cash</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Exchange loss on rupiah (section 7)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Separate line, (375)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>ECL (section 7)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Not a flow; a non-cash item in an indirect-method reconciliation</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Interest and dividends are each disclosed separately and classified consistently (para. 31). Today a non-financial entity may put interest paid in operating or financing, interest and dividends received in operating or investing, and dividends paid in financing or operating (paras. 33–34). That choice ends with IFRS 18. Its amendments to IAS 7 require an entity like PHE, whose main business is not investing or lending, to classify interest and dividends paid as financing and interest and dividends received as investing, for periods beginning on or after 1 January 2027. Indonesia's PSAK 118, ratified by DSAK IAI on 28 May 2025, takes effect on the same date.</p><h2>9. Cash across industries</h2><p>The definition is the same everywhere. What differs is which flows are operating, what may be netted, and whose money it is.</p><p><strong>Banks.</strong> For a bank, deposits from customers and loans to them are the business itself, so interest paid and received are usually operating (para. 33), and deposits with fixed maturities, placements with other banks and customer loans may be reported net (para. 24). A bank's own cash and cash equivalents usually include its current account at the central bank. In Indonesia that account carries the statutory reserve requirement (<em>giro wajib minimum</em>, GWM), a minimum balance Bank Indonesia requires against deposits; banks commonly keep it within cash and cash equivalents even though it cannot all be spent.</p><p><strong>Agents holding other people's money.</strong> Receipts that reflect the customer's activities more than the entity's may be shown net, for example "funds held for customers by an investment entity" and "rents collected on behalf of, and paid over to, the owners of properties" (paras. 22–23). For example (illustration), a property manager collects rent of 900 for owners, keeps a 10 percent fee and pays over the rest.</p><blockquote><p>Gross presentation: receipts 900, payments to owners (810)</p><p>Net presentation: fee received 900 × 10% = 90</p></blockquote><p>Whether the money held belongs in the agent's cash at all depends on whose account it is and whether the agent can draw it.</p><p><strong>Cryptocurrency.</strong> A holding of cryptocurrency fails both tests. It is neither currency on hand nor a bank deposit, and its value is too volatile for a cash equivalent. The IFRS Interpretations Committee concluded in June 2019 that it is inventory if held for sale in the ordinary course of business and an intangible asset under PSAK 238 (formerly PSAK 19, IAS 38) otherwise.</p><p><strong>Groups with subsidiaries under exchange controls.</strong> Cash held by a subsidiary in a country whose laws stop it being sent to the parent stays in the group's cash, but PSAK 207 requires disclosure of significant balances "not available for use by the group", with a commentary by management (paras. 48–49). PHE holds small balances in currencies such as the Algerian dinar (1,721 at the end of 2024, Note 6, Daftar 5/92, PDF p. 109); the FS do not say whether any of its cash is subject to such controls.</p><p><strong>Group cash pooling.</strong> In a <em>physical</em> pool, balances are swept daily into one master account; in a <em>notional</em> pool, they stay in separate accounts and the bank pays interest on their net position. Showing the pool net needs both a set-off right and an intention to settle net (PSAK 232.42). In 2016 the IFRS Interpretations Committee looked at a notional pool that had the right but whose members kept using their own accounts before each net settlement. It concluded that the group could not claim an intention to settle the period-end balances net, so they stay gross. Other pools need their own judgement.</p><h2>10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 6</h2><p>PSAK 207 asks for the policy for determining cash equivalents, the components of cash and cash equivalents, a reconciliation to the balance sheet, and significant balances not available for use (paras. 45–48). PHE gives components, banks and currencies (Note 6, Daftar 5/90–5/93, PDF pp. 107–110).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash on hand</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>171</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>200</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash in banks</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,476,825</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4,253,727</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Time deposits</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>137,323</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>764,812</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Cash and cash equivalents</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>2,614,318</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>5,018,739</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The 2024 components add to 2,614,319, one thousand above the printed total through rounding. Dollar accounts make up 2,048,144 of the 2,476,825 of cash in banks (82.7 percent), and 2,386,340 of the line (91.3 percent) sits with government-related banks (Note 44b, Daftar 5/182, PDF p. 199). Time deposits fell from 15.2 to 5.3 percent of the line, while US dollar deposit rates rose from 0.22–1.10 percent to 0.75–6.92 percent (Note 6, Daftar 5/93, PDF p. 110).</p><p>The cash flow statement explains the fall (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17).</p><blockquote><p>Net cash from operating activities = 5,629,285</p><p>Net cash used in investing activities = (3,798,984), of which purchase of oil and gas properties (3,933,616)</p><p>Net cash used in financing activities = (4,214,984), of which dividends paid (2,117,827)</p><p>Net decrease = (2,384,682), as printed; the three subtotals add to (2,384,683)</p><p>Effect of exchange-rate changes = (19,739)</p><p>Closing cash = 5,018,739 − 2,384,682 − 19,739 = 2,614,318</p></blockquote><p>Operating cash covered the net investing outflow with 1,830,301 to spare (5,629,285 − 3,798,984). Financing outflows of 4,214,984, mainly dividends of 2,117,827 and loan repayments of 1,679,292, were larger than that surplus, and the difference came out of the opening balance. A common liquidity measure compares cash with current liabilities.</p><blockquote><p>Cash ratio = cash and cash equivalents / current liabilities = 2,614,318 / 6,158,999 = 42.4% (2024); 5,018,739 / 6,017,916 = 83.4% (2023)</p></blockquote><p>Against 2023 the picture changes. Financing outflows were 310,096 higher (3,904,888 → 4,214,984) and investing outflows 370,325 lower (4,169,309 → 3,798,984), while operating cash fell by 3,034,495 (8,663,780 → 5,629,285). A pattern of spending that left cash 589,583 higher in 2023 drew it down in 2024 because operating receipts were lower. The ratio shows the size of the buffer. Reading why it moved takes the cash flow statement, and both years of it.</p><h2>11. Upstream note: cash in joint operations</h2><p>Many upstream blocks are joint operations, in which each party recognises its share of the assets held jointly under PSAK 111 <em>Joint Arrangements</em> (formerly PSAK 66, IFRS 11). A non-operator usually pays the operator ahead of spending, a <em>cash call</em>. The payment leaves the partner's cash and becomes an advance until the operator reports how it was spent. PHE shows cash call advances of 76,196 among current advances at the end of 2024, against 132,275 a year earlier (Note 15, Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123).</p><h2>12. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/restricted-cash">restricted-cash</a>: balances the group cannot withdraw on demand, current and non-current.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/long-term-bank-loans-non-current">short-term-bank-loans</a>: where overdrafts and pooling balances sit on the balance sheet.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current">advances-prepayments-current</a>: cash calls paid to operators.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/finance-cost">finance-income</a>: interest on bank balances and time deposits.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/foreign-exchange-loss-net">foreign-exchange-loss-net</a>: retranslation of non-dollar balances.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>That large Indonesian banks include their current account at Bank Indonesia (<em>giro pada Bank Indonesia</em>) in cash and cash equivalents is confirmed only through a secondary source (CNBC Indonesia on BRI, Mandiri, BCA and BNI); how much of that balance is the GWM, and whether all banks follow the same policy, was not checked in a bank's FS.</p></li><li><p>How a non-operator presents its share of cash still held in an operator's joint account (as cash, or within advances until the billing statement) depends on the agreement and reporting practice; PHE's FS do not say, and IFRS 11.20 was not opened.</p></li><li><p>The IFRS 18 amendments to IAS 7 are confirmed through secondary sources (KPMG, BDO), and PSAK 118's ratification and effective date through IAI. Whether DSAK has issued the matching amendment to PSAK 207 with the same wording was not confirmed.</p></li><li><p>The ECL mechanics (low-credit-risk simplification, twelve-month measure) and PSAK 232.42 are stated from knowledge of IFRS 9 and IAS 32; their paragraphs were not opened, although the 2016 cash-pooling decision restates para. 42(b).</p></li><li><p>Kieso's classification table and the imprest and reconciliation formats come from knowledge of the textbook; no copy was opened.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 3a (Daftar 5/29, PDF p. 46); Note 3f (Daftar 5/38, PDF p. 55); Note 3r (Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83); Note 6 (Daftar 5/90–5/93, PDF pp. 107–110); Note 7 (Daftar 5/93, PDF p. 110); Note 15 (Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123); Note 26 (Daftar 5/125, PDF p. 142); Note 44b (Daftar 5/182, PDF p. 199); Note 49c (Daftar 5/232, PDF p. 249). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).</p></li><li><p>The order follows Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting, IFRS Edition</em>, chapter "Cash and Receivables" and its appendix on cash controls. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED (textbook not opened).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 207 <em>Laporan Arus Kas</em> (IAS 7 <em>Statement of Cash Flows</em>), paras. 6–9, 16, 19, 22–28, 31, 33–34, 43–49. VERIFIED (web, IAS 7 text as published by the IFRS Foundation, copy at moodle2.units.it, opened and extracted).</p></li><li><p>IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision <em>Demand Deposits with Restrictions on Use arising from a Contract with a Third Party (IAS 7)</em>, April 2022. VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org PDF, opened and extracted).</p></li><li><p>IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision <em>Holdings of Cryptocurrencies</em>, June 2019. VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org listing and IAS 38 annotation; full PDF not opened).</p></li><li><p>IFRS Interpretations Committee, <em>IAS 32 Offsetting and Cash Pooling Arrangements</em>, March 2016. VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org agenda decision PDF, opened and extracted).</p></li><li><p>KPMG, "IFRS 18 changes to the statement of cash flows" (2026), and BDO IFRB 2026/05; IAI, <em>DSAK Terkini</em> Vol. 7 (2025) on PSAK 118, ratified 28 May 2025, effective 1 January 2027. VERIFIED (web; IFRS 18 text not opened).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 221 (IAS 21), PSAK 109 (IFRS 9), PSAK 232 (IAS 32), PSAK 111 (IFRS 11), PSAK 238 (IAS 38). Numbers and titles VERIFIED (web, IAI, <em>Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia</em>); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>CNBC Indonesia, "Simak Gerak Saham 7 Emiten BUMN…", 10 March 2020, on the cash and cash equivalents of BRI, Mandiri, BCA and BNI (cash plus <em>giro pada Bank Indonesia</em>). VERIFIED (web, secondary).</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash is money the entity can spend today, meaning cash on hand and demand deposits. A cash equivalent is an investment held for short-term cash needs that converts to a known amount with insignificant risk, which in practice means a maturity of three months or less from the date it was acquired. Everything else that looks like cash, from post-dated cheques to six-month deposits, belongs on another line.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash is the asset most exposed to error and theft, so its accounting is mostly control, through an imprest petty cash fund and a bank reconciliation that separates items the bank has not yet recorded from items the books have not yet recorded. Only the second kind needs a journal.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The balance sheet shows where cash ended up; the cash flow statement (PSAK 207) explains how it got there, through operating, investing and financing flows plus a separate line for exchange-rate effects. Moves between cash and cash equivalents are not cash flows, and an overdraft that is part of day-to-day cash management can count as negative cash in the flow statement while staying a liability on the balance sheet.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report cash and cash equivalents of 2,614,318 at 31 December 2024 against 5,018,739 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The line is 8.6 percent of total assets and 32.5 percent of current assets, down from 16.2 and 50.8 percent. This page uses those balances as the starting point for its examples.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading it a student should be able to say what belongs in the line, record the entries that keep it accurate, and trace a change in cash through the cash flow statement.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. The order follows the chapter on cash in Kieso's ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IFRS edition) and the structure of PSAK 207.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. What counts as cash","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash is the one asset whose value needs no estimate, and the definition protects that. PSAK 207 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Statement of Cash Flows","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 2, IAS 7) defines ","type":"text"},{"text":"cash","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" as cash on hand and demand deposits (para. 6). A ","type":"text"},{"text":"demand deposit","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is a bank balance the holder can withdraw at any time without notice or penalty, such as a current account (","type":"text"},{"text":"giro","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":") or a savings account.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The test is whether the item is money now. Kieso's textbook works through the items a cashier finds in the drawer, and they sort into three groups.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Item","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Where it goes","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Why","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Coins, banknotes, cheques and money orders received but not yet deposited","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The bank will accept them as money at once","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current accounts and savings accounts","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Withdrawable on demand","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Petty cash and change funds","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Money on hand, kept for small payments","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Post-dated cheques from customers","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade receivables","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Not payable until a later date","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cheques returned unpaid (NSF, \"not sufficient funds\")","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Receivables","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A claim on the customer, not money","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Employee IOUs and travel advances","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other receivables or prepayments","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Money will come back as cash or as receipts for expenses","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Postage stamps, prepaid vouchers","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Prepaid expenses","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Already spent on a future service","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Time deposits","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash equivalents or investments","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Depends on maturity at acquisition (section 2)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Overdrawn bank accounts","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Liabilities","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Money owed to the bank (section 4)","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's Note 6 has three components, cash on hand of 171, cash in banks of 2,476,825 and time deposits of 137,323 (Daftar 5/90, PDF p. 107). For a group with more than 2.6 billion dollars of cash, 171 of notes and coins is almost nothing, the pattern of a business that settles almost everything through banks.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Cash equivalents and the three-month rule","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A cash equivalent is defined by its purpose and by the certainty of what it converts into. PSAK 207 calls cash equivalents \"short-term, highly liquid investments that are readily convertible to known amounts of cash and which are subject to an insignificant risk of changes in value\" (para. 6), held \"for the purpose of meeting short-term cash commitments rather than for investment or other purposes\" (para. 7). Two consequences follow.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Equity investments are excluded","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" unless they are cash equivalents in substance, such as preference shares bought shortly before a fixed redemption date (para. 7). A listed share can be sold in a minute, but nobody knows for how much.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Maturity is measured from the date of acquisition.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" The standard says an investment \"normally\" qualifies only with a maturity of \"three months or less from the date of acquisition\" (para. 7). A six-month deposit therefore never becomes a cash equivalent, even when only one month is left. A two-year government bond bought with two months to run, for cash management, can qualify from the day it is bought.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's policy uses the same boundary, admitting time deposits \"with maturity periods of three months or less at the time of placement which are not used as collateral or are not restricted\" (Note 3f, Daftar 5/38, PDF p. 55).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), on 1 October 2024 a PHE subsidiary has 100,000 more than it needs for the next quarter. Its treasury places 40,000 in a three-month USD deposit maturing on 1 January 2025 and 60,000 in a six-month deposit maturing on 1 April 2025, both at 5.0 percent a year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Three-month deposit: maturity at placement = 3 months, so it is a cash equivalent","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Six-month deposit: maturity at placement = 6 months, so it is a short-term investment","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest accrued at 31 December = 40,000 × 5.0% × 3/12 = 500 (three-month); 60,000 × 5.0% × 3/12 = 750 (six-month)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Three-month deposit","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Six-month deposit","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1 October","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash equivalents, time deposits 40,000 / Cr Cash in banks 40,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Short-term investments, time deposits 60,000 / Cr Cash in banks 60,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 December","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Interest receivable 500 / Cr Finance income 500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Interest receivable 750 / Cr Finance income 750","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash flow statement, 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"No flow: a move within cash and cash equivalents (para. 9)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Investing outflow of 60,000","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Both deposits are measured at ","type":"text"},{"text":"amortised cost","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", principal plus interest accrued at the deposit's own rate; here the accrued interest is shown as a receivable, though some entities add it to the deposit. They differ only in the line they sit on, and that decides whether the placement is a cash flow at all.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Restricted cash and compensating balances","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A demand deposit stays cash even when a contract says what it may be spent on; it leaves the line only when the entity cannot withdraw it on demand. The IFRS Interpretations Committee concluded in April 2022 that restrictions on use agreed with a third party do not stop a deposit being cash \"unless those restrictions change the nature of the deposit in a way that it would no longer meet the definition of cash\". A restriction that leaves the money withdrawable is handled by disclosure.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A balance the entity cannot withdraw, because a bank blocks it as collateral or because a third party such as a regulator or a lender must consent to each withdrawal, is ","type":"text"},{"text":"restricted cash","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". Kieso's ","type":"text"},{"text":"compensating balance","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", a minimum deposit a lender requires as part of a loan, is the textbook case when the agreement legally blocks it. PHE shows restricted cash on two separate lines, current 154,217 and non-current 1,890,493 (Note 7, Daftar 5/93, PDF p. 110), and the companion page ","type":"text"},{"text":"restricted-cash","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/restricted-cash","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":" treats it in full.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Bank overdrafts and offsetting","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An overdraft is a loan from the bank, so it is a liability. Two different rules then decide whether it may be netted against positive balances, one for the balance sheet and one for the cash flow statement.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"On the balance sheet, PSAK 232 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Financial Instruments: Presentation","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32) allows a financial asset and a financial liability to be shown net only when the entity has a legally enforceable right to set them off and intends to settle net or simultaneously (para. 42). Accounts at different banks almost never meet the test. Accounts at the same bank meet it only if the agreement gives a set-off right and the entity intends to settle net.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In the cash flow statement, PSAK 207 makes an exception to the rule that bank borrowings are financing. Overdrafts \"repayable on demand\" that \"form an integral part of an entity's cash management\" are a negative component of cash and cash equivalents, typically where \"the bank balance often fluctuates from being positive to overdrawn\" (para. 8).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), at 31 December 2024 a PHE subsidiary has 5,000 in its account at bank X and an operating account at bank Y overdrawn by 1,200. The account at Y swings between positive and overdrawn as invoices fall due before monthly sales receipts arrive, and bank Y can call the overdraft at any time.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Balance sheet","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash flow statement","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Rule","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 232.42: no right of set-off between banks X and Y","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 207.8: overdraft repayable on demand and part of cash management","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash and cash equivalents","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5,000 − 1,200 = 3,800","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Overdraft","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,200 within short-term bank loans","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Not a financing flow; drawing and repaying it are movements within cash","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The note on cash must then reconcile the 3,800 in the cash flow statement with the 5,000 on the balance sheet (para. 45). PHE's short-term bank loans of 15,775, part of which PDSI draws through a notional pooling arrangement, are shown as loans (Note 26, Daftar 5/125, PDF p. 142); its cash flow statement closes at the same 2,614,318 as the balance sheet, so no overdraft is netted into cash.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Controlling cash: petty cash","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Small payments are made from a fund kept on hand, and the ","type":"text"},{"text":"imprest system","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" keeps it under control by fixing its size. A custodian holds a set amount. Each payment is supported by a receipt, so receipts plus remaining cash should always equal the fixed amount. When the fund runs low it is topped back up to that amount, and the expenses are recorded only then, from the receipts.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a PHE field office sets up a petty cash fund of 8.0 (USD 8 thousand). By month end the custodian holds receipts for local transport 3.1, meals for a site crew 2.4 and office supplies 1.6, and counts 0.8 of cash.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Receipts + cash = 3.1 + 2.4 + 1.6 + 0.8 = 7.9, against a fund of 8.0, so 0.1 is short","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Replenishment = 8.0 − 0.8 = 7.2","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Establish the fund","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Replenish at month end","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Petty cash 8.0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Transport expense 3.1, Dr Meals expense 2.4, Dr Office supplies expense 1.6, Dr Cash over and short 0.1","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cr Cash in banks 8.0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cr Cash in banks 7.2","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The petty cash account moves only when the fund's fixed size is changed. Setting up and replenishing the fund move money between two cash accounts, so neither is a cash flow; the outflow is what the custodian paid out. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Cash over and short","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is an expense when short and other income when over, and a shortage that recurs is a control finding. Kieso's other controls follow the same logic: separate handling cash from recording it, deposit receipts intact daily, and pay everything else through the bank.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Controlling cash: the bank reconciliation","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The bank's record and the entity's record of the same account rarely agree at month end, and the ","type":"text"},{"text":"bank reconciliation","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" explains why. Each side is adjusted for what it does not yet know, until both reach the same correct balance.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Bank side: balance per bank + deposits in transit − outstanding cheques ± bank errors = correct balance","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Book side: balance per books + collections and interest credited by the bank − bank charges − returned (NSF) cheques ± book errors = correct balance","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary's USD account at 31 December 2024.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The bank statement shows 12,480. A deposit of 1,350 made on 31 December reaches the bank in January (deposit in transit). Cheques of 2,940 issued to vendors have not been presented (outstanding cheques).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The books show 9,912. The bank has credited interest of 62 and a joint-venture partner's cash call of 800 paid directly into the account, and has deducted charges of 4. A cheque for 150 from a scrap buyer came back unpaid. A vendor payment of 1,250 was recorded in the books as 1,520.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Bank side = 12,480 + 1,350 − 2,940 = 10,890","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Book side = 9,912 + 62 + 800 − 4 − 150 + (1,520 − 1,250) = 9,912 + 62 + 800 − 4 − 150 + 270 = 10,890","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Both sides reach 10,890, which is the cash the balance sheet should show. Bank-side items need no entry, because the books are already right about them; the bank will catch up in January. Book-side items each need a journal.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Item","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Journal","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest credited","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash in banks 62 / Cr Finance income 62","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Partner's cash call received","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash in banks 800 / Cr Receivable from joint operation partners 800","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Bank charges","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Bank charges expense 4 / Cr Cash in banks 4","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Returned cheque","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Other receivables, scrap buyer 150 / Cr Cash in banks 150","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Recording error","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash in banks 270 / Cr Trade payables 270","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Book balance after the five entries = 9,912 + 62 + 800 − 4 − 150 + 270 = 10,890","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The recording error is credited to trade payables because the original entry debited payables with 1,520 when only 1,250 was settled.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Foreign currency cash and credit losses on bank balances","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash in another currency is a ","type":"text"},{"text":"monetary item","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", an amount fixed in units of that currency, so its value in the functional currency moves with the exchange rate. PSAK 221 ","type":"text"},{"text":"The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 10, IAS 21) retranslates monetary items at the closing rate at each reporting date and takes the difference to profit or loss. PHE's functional currency is the US dollar for every entity except Elnusa, and its monetary items are translated at the closing JISDOR rate, Bank Indonesia's reference rate (Note 3r, Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83). PHE held rupiah bank accounts of 375,921 at the end of 2024 (Note 6, Daftar 5/91, PDF p. 108).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a subsidiary holds IDR 154,000 million throughout 2024 to pay local vendors, while the rupiah weakens from 15,400 to 16,000 per dollar (constructed rates).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"USD value at 1 January = IDR 154,000 million / 15,400 = USD 10.0 million = 10,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"USD value at 31 December = IDR 154,000 million / 16,000 = 9,625","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Exchange loss = 10,000 − 9,625 = 375","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Foreign exchange loss 375 / Cr Cash in banks 375","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"No money left the account, so the 375 is not a cash flow. PSAK 207 still shows it, on a separate line after the three activities, so that opening cash plus flows plus the exchange effect equals closing cash (para. 28). If flows had occurred during the year, that line would also absorb the difference between translating them at transaction-date rates and at the closing rate (paras. 25, 28).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Bank balances are also financial assets at amortised cost, so PSAK 109 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Financial Instruments","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9) requires an ","type":"text"},{"text":"expected credit loss","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (ECL), the probability-weighted loss if the bank fails. For a bank with low credit risk the entity may assume credit risk has not increased significantly and measure only twelve-month expected losses. For example (illustration), 1,000,000 at a domestic bank with a 0.05 percent one-year probability of default and a 45 percent loss if default occurs.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"ECL = 1,000,000 × 0.05% × 45% = 225","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Impairment loss on financial assets 225 / Cr Allowance for credit losses, cash in banks 225","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The amount is usually immaterial, which is why cash notes seldom show it. PHE reports that 4,182,445 of its 4,521,534 of cash in banks and restricted cash sits with Pefindo-rated counterparties, 338,966 with Fitch, Moody's or S&P-rated ones and 123 with unrated ones (Note 49c, Daftar 5/232, PDF p. 249).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. From the balance to the cash flow statement","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The cash flow statement exists because two balances cannot explain the change between them. PSAK 207 sorts every cash flow into three activities (para. 6).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Operating","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": the principal revenue-producing activities, and anything that is not investing or financing.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Investing","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": the acquisition and disposal of long-term assets and other investments not included in cash equivalents. Only spending that results in a recognised asset may be classified here (para. 16).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Financing","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": changes in equity and borrowings.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Movements between items that are all cash or cash equivalents are not flows (para. 9). Operating cash flows can be shown by the ","type":"text"},{"text":"direct method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", which lists gross receipts and payments, or the ","type":"text"},{"text":"indirect method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", which starts from profit and adjusts for non-cash items and working capital. The standard encourages the direct method (para. 19), and PHE uses it (Note 3a, Daftar 5/29, PDF p. 46).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The examples above land in the statement as follows.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Item from this page","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash flow treatment","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Three-month deposit (section 2)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"No flow","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Six-month deposit (section 2)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Investing outflow 60,000, and an investing inflow on maturity","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Overdraft at bank Y (section 4)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Negative cash; no financing flow","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Petty cash spending (section 5)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Operating outflow 7.2 (payments 7.1 and the 0.1 shortage), recorded at replenishment; setting up and topping up the fund are moves within cash","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Exchange loss on rupiah (section 7)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Separate line, (375)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"ECL (section 7)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Not a flow; a non-cash item in an indirect-method reconciliation","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest and dividends are each disclosed separately and classified consistently (para. 31). Today a non-financial entity may put interest paid in operating or financing, interest and dividends received in operating or investing, and dividends paid in financing or operating (paras. 33–34). That choice ends with IFRS 18. Its amendments to IAS 7 require an entity like PHE, whose main business is not investing or lending, to classify interest and dividends paid as financing and interest and dividends received as investing, for periods beginning on or after 1 January 2027. Indonesia's PSAK 118, ratified by DSAK IAI on 28 May 2025, takes effect on the same date.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Cash across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The definition is the same everywhere. What differs is which flows are operating, what may be netted, and whose money it is.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Banks.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" For a bank, deposits from customers and loans to them are the business itself, so interest paid and received are usually operating (para. 33), and deposits with fixed maturities, placements with other banks and customer loans may be reported net (para. 24). A bank's own cash and cash equivalents usually include its current account at the central bank. In Indonesia that account carries the statutory reserve requirement (","type":"text"},{"text":"giro wajib minimum","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", GWM), a minimum balance Bank Indonesia requires against deposits; banks commonly keep it within cash and cash equivalents even though it cannot all be spent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Agents holding other people's money.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Receipts that reflect the customer's activities more than the entity's may be shown net, for example \"funds held for customers by an investment entity\" and \"rents collected on behalf of, and paid over to, the owners of properties\" (paras. 22–23). For example (illustration), a property manager collects rent of 900 for owners, keeps a 10 percent fee and pays over the rest.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gross presentation: receipts 900, payments to owners (810)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net presentation: fee received 900 × 10% = 90","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Whether the money held belongs in the agent's cash at all depends on whose account it is and whether the agent can draw it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cryptocurrency.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A holding of cryptocurrency fails both tests. It is neither currency on hand nor a bank deposit, and its value is too volatile for a cash equivalent. The IFRS Interpretations Committee concluded in June 2019 that it is inventory if held for sale in the ordinary course of business and an intangible asset under PSAK 238 (formerly PSAK 19, IAS 38) otherwise.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Groups with subsidiaries under exchange controls.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Cash held by a subsidiary in a country whose laws stop it being sent to the parent stays in the group's cash, but PSAK 207 requires disclosure of significant balances \"not available for use by the group\", with a commentary by management (paras. 48–49). PHE holds small balances in currencies such as the Algerian dinar (1,721 at the end of 2024, Note 6, Daftar 5/92, PDF p. 109); the FS do not say whether any of its cash is subject to such controls.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Group cash pooling.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" In a ","type":"text"},{"text":"physical","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" pool, balances are swept daily into one master account; in a ","type":"text"},{"text":"notional","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" pool, they stay in separate accounts and the bank pays interest on their net position. Showing the pool net needs both a set-off right and an intention to settle net (PSAK 232.42). In 2016 the IFRS Interpretations Committee looked at a notional pool that had the right but whose members kept using their own accounts before each net settlement. It concluded that the group could not claim an intention to settle the period-end balances net, so they stay gross. Other pools need their own judgement.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 6","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 207 asks for the policy for determining cash equivalents, the components of cash and cash equivalents, a reconciliation to the balance sheet, and significant balances not available for use (paras. 45–48). PHE gives components, banks and currencies (Note 6, Daftar 5/90–5/93, PDF pp. 107–110).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash on hand","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"171","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"200","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash in banks","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,476,825","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,253,727","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Time deposits","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"137,323","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"764,812","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash and cash equivalents","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,614,318","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5,018,739","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 2024 components add to 2,614,319, one thousand above the printed total through rounding. Dollar accounts make up 2,048,144 of the 2,476,825 of cash in banks (82.7 percent), and 2,386,340 of the line (91.3 percent) sits with government-related banks (Note 44b, Daftar 5/182, PDF p. 199). Time deposits fell from 15.2 to 5.3 percent of the line, while US dollar deposit rates rose from 0.22–1.10 percent to 0.75–6.92 percent (Note 6, Daftar 5/93, PDF p. 110).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The cash flow statement explains the fall (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net cash from operating activities = 5,629,285","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net cash used in investing activities = (3,798,984), of which purchase of oil and gas properties (3,933,616)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net cash used in financing activities = (4,214,984), of which dividends paid (2,117,827)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net decrease = (2,384,682), as printed; the three subtotals add to (2,384,683)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Effect of exchange-rate changes = (19,739)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Closing cash = 5,018,739 − 2,384,682 − 19,739 = 2,614,318","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Operating cash covered the net investing outflow with 1,830,301 to spare (5,629,285 − 3,798,984). Financing outflows of 4,214,984, mainly dividends of 2,117,827 and loan repayments of 1,679,292, were larger than that surplus, and the difference came out of the opening balance. A common liquidity measure compares cash with current liabilities.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash ratio = cash and cash equivalents / current liabilities = 2,614,318 / 6,158,999 = 42.4% (2024); 5,018,739 / 6,017,916 = 83.4% (2023)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Against 2023 the picture changes. Financing outflows were 310,096 higher (3,904,888 → 4,214,984) and investing outflows 370,325 lower (4,169,309 → 3,798,984), while operating cash fell by 3,034,495 (8,663,780 → 5,629,285). A pattern of spending that left cash 589,583 higher in 2023 drew it down in 2024 because operating receipts were lower. The ratio shows the size of the buffer. Reading why it moved takes the cash flow statement, and both years of it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. Upstream note: cash in joint operations","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Many upstream blocks are joint operations, in which each party recognises its share of the assets held jointly under PSAK 111 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Joint Arrangements","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 66, IFRS 11). A non-operator usually pays the operator ahead of spending, a ","type":"text"},{"text":"cash call","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". The payment leaves the partner's cash and becomes an advance until the operator reports how it was spent. PHE shows cash call advances of 76,196 among current advances at the end of 2024, against 132,275 a year earlier (Note 15, Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"restricted-cash","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/restricted-cash","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": balances the group cannot withdraw on demand, current and non-current.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"short-term-bank-loans","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/long-term-bank-loans-non-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where overdrafts and pooling balances sit on the balance sheet.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"advances-prepayments-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": cash calls paid to operators.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"finance-income","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/finance-cost","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": interest on bank balances and time deposits.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"foreign-exchange-loss-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/foreign-exchange-loss-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": retranslation of non-dollar balances.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That large Indonesian banks include their current account at Bank Indonesia (","type":"text"},{"text":"giro pada Bank Indonesia","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":") in cash and cash equivalents is confirmed only through a secondary source (CNBC Indonesia on BRI, Mandiri, BCA and BNI); how much of that balance is the GWM, and whether all banks follow the same policy, was not checked in a bank's FS.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"How a non-operator presents its share of cash still held in an operator's joint account (as cash, or within advances until the billing statement) depends on the agreement and reporting practice; PHE's FS do not say, and IFRS 11.20 was not opened.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The IFRS 18 amendments to IAS 7 are confirmed through secondary sources (KPMG, BDO), and PSAK 118's ratification and effective date through IAI. Whether DSAK has issued the matching amendment to PSAK 207 with the same wording was not confirmed.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The ECL mechanics (low-credit-risk simplification, twelve-month measure) and PSAK 232.42 are stated from knowledge of IFRS 9 and IAS 32; their paragraphs were not opened, although the 2016 cash-pooling decision restates para. 42(b).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso's classification table and the imprest and reconciliation formats come from knowledge of the textbook; no copy was opened.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 3a (Daftar 5/29, PDF p. 46); Note 3f (Daftar 5/38, PDF p. 55); Note 3r (Daftar 5/66, PDF p. 83); Note 6 (Daftar 5/90–5/93, PDF pp. 107–110); Note 7 (Daftar 5/93, PDF p. 110); Note 15 (Daftar 5/106, PDF p. 123); Note 26 (Daftar 5/125, PDF p. 142); Note 44b (Daftar 5/182, PDF p. 199); Note 49c (Daftar 5/232, PDF p. 249). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The order follows Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting, IFRS Edition","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", chapter \"Cash and Receivables\" and its appendix on cash controls. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED (textbook not opened).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 207 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Laporan Arus Kas","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 7 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Statement of Cash Flows","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"), paras. 6–9, 16, 19, 22–28, 31, 33–34, 43–49. VERIFIED (web, IAS 7 text as published by the IFRS Foundation, copy at moodle2.units.it, opened and extracted).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision ","type":"text"},{"text":"Demand Deposits with Restrictions on Use arising from a Contract with a Third Party (IAS 7)","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", April 2022. VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org PDF, opened and extracted).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision ","type":"text"},{"text":"Holdings of Cryptocurrencies","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", June 2019. VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org listing and IAS 38 annotation; full PDF not opened).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"IFRS Interpretations Committee, ","type":"text"},{"text":"IAS 32 Offsetting and Cash Pooling Arrangements","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", March 2016. VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org agenda decision PDF, opened and extracted).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"KPMG, \"IFRS 18 changes to the statement of cash flows\" (2026), and BDO IFRB 2026/05; IAI, ","type":"text"},{"text":"DSAK Terkini","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" Vol. 7 (2025) on PSAK 118, ratified 28 May 2025, effective 1 January 2027. VERIFIED (web; IFRS 18 text not opened).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 221 (IAS 21), PSAK 109 (IFRS 9), PSAK 232 (IAS 32), PSAK 111 (IFRS 11), PSAK 238 (IAS 38). Numbers and titles VERIFIED (web, IAI, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"CNBC Indonesia, \"Simak Gerak Saham 7 Emiten BUMN…\", 10 March 2020, on the cash and cash equivalents of BRI, Mandiri, BCA and BNI (cash plus ","type":"text"},{"text":"giro pada Bank Indonesia","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"). VERIFIED (web, secondary).","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of the cash line from definition to the cash flow statement, worked through PHE's balances and set beside the industries with their own rules","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["Cash is money the entity can spend today, meaning cash on hand and demand deposits. A cash equivalent is an investment held for short-term cash needs that converts to a known amount with insignificant risk, which in practice means a maturity of three months or less from the date it was acquired. Everything else that looks like cash, from post-dated cheques to six-month deposits, belongs on another line.","Cash is the asset most exposed to error and theft, so its accounting is mostly control, through an imprest petty cash fund and a bank reconciliation that separates items the bank has not yet recorded from items the books have not yet recorded. Only the second kind needs a journal.","The balance sheet shows where cash ended up; the cash flow statement (PSAK 207) explains how it got there, through operating, investing and financing flows plus a separate line for exchange-rate effects. Moves between cash and cash equivalents are not cash flows, and an overdraft that is part of day-to-day cash management can count as negative cash in the flow statement while staying a liability on the balance sheet."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = 'd64f7bdf-ee33-4484-98bf-4ff3c136e469' AND updated_at = '2026-10-12T09:47:08.318531+00:00'::timestamptz;
